    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46199": {
      "title": "Business Development Manager",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Business Development Manager Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Business Development Manager Apply for job Job reference: CIX-032564 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Cultural Institutions Location: Jodrell Bank, Cheshire Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: JBCE Operations Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 28/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent starting as soon as possible School/Directorate: Jodrell Bank Centre for Engagement Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Business Development Manager is a pivotal role for Jodrell Bank Centre for Engagement (JBCE), responsible for driving and maximising earned income across the centre from venue hire, groups, filming and other revenue generating initiatives at a unique, UNESCO world heritage site. The role is responsible for leading and driving all sales activity, together with the development and delivery of the centre’s business strategy which proactively generates new business and income across corporate meetings, events and private dining, together with weddings, group bookings and filming opportunities. Commercial activity will be successfully converted, planned and delivered through building strong relationships with corporate clients, agencies, private bookers and key partners, together with colleagues across JBCE. In this role, you will: Curate a \"unique\" commercial offer & experience: develop premium packages and offers which reflect Jodrell’s location and unique facilities for corporate and group bookings. Lead an inspired team: Motivate and empower our Events Coordinator and team of Casual Event Assistants to deliver professional client liaison and exceptional customer service when attending site for their booking/event. Commercial focus: Have a keen focus on commercial success, driving revenue and maximising profitability, whilst being mindful of other activity across a busy visitor and educational site. Partner for success: Collaborate with the Head of Marketing & Communications, together with the Catering & Hospitality Manager to help plan marketing and sales initiatives to showcase the facilities and catering across Jodrell Bank for bookings and events. Contribute to our strategic vision: Set and track key performance indicators for commercial business, check costs, analyse budgets, and contribute to the annual business plans for Venue Hire, group bookings and other income streams. Jodrell Bank is a vibrant centre for scientific discovery, education and inspiration. You will join a passionate team and play a key role in shaping an outstanding visitor experience. Our Values Jodrell Bank Centre for Engagement (JBCE) is a Cultural Institution of the University of Manchester. We value: Inclusivity: we support participation for diverse audiences, communities and staff, seeking to connect people with Jodrell Bank and with each other. Furthermore, we strive to create an inclusive culture by recognising each other’s differences and strengths. Integrity: we care deeply about the impact of our work, and we hold ourselves to high standards. We value honesty and integrity, ensuring that our practices are always responsible, ethical and sustainable. Empowerment: we empower our audiences, communities, our staff and our volunteers to enable confidence. Support: Our success rests on the achievements of others, and in turn we aim to provide the tools and the support needed to inspire others. All colleagues are expected to share this vision and help create a rewarding and fulfilling working environment. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Adam Woof Email: adam.woof@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Supporting Documents save_alt CIX-032564 Business Development Manager FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46139": {
      "title": "Research Associate on Pinching Antenna Systems",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate on Pinching Antenna Systems Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate on Pinching Antenna Systems Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032480 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 20/10/2026 Contract Duration: 36 Months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Job Description The University of Manchester (www.manchester.ac.uk) enjoys a global reputation for its research and its innovative approach to learning, with a £1 billion investment in facilities, staff and new engineering buildings that fully opened in 2022. This builds on our tradition of success that stretches back over 180 years. We are at the forefront of the search for solutions to some of the world's most pressing challenges, with strong collaborative links with industry and public services. The Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering (EEE) at the University of Manchester is one of the largest departments in its discipline in the UK. The Communications and Signal Processing research group has the combined strength of communication systems, microwave engineering, and signal processing. With 13 academic staff and over 70 PhD students, research associates and visitors, it is one of the largest groups in the UK specialising in mobile wireless networks, antennas and propagation, RF and microwave sensors and tomographic systems, radar, signal analysis and processing. The post is funded by a grant from the Engineering and Physical Sciences Research Council (EPSRC) on “Pinching Antenna Systems (PASS) – A New Paradigm for Wireless Network Designs”. The aim of this project is to investigate a new type of flexible-antenna systems, PASS, whose core concept is to activate pinching antennas by applying small dielectric particles, e.g., plastic pinches, onto a dielectric waveguide. A prototype demonstrated that strong LoS communication links can be established by this simple, low-cost pinching mechanism. With a stepper motor, a pinching antenna can be dynamically deployed near the receiver to establish the strong LoS link, achieving ultra-high spectral and energy efficiency. This architecture creates a new paradigm for future mobile networks and is currently one of the most active research topics in wireless communication. Purpose of the Job This post will support a recently awarded grant conducting ambitious research on the development of PASS. The scope of work ranges from the development of a theoretical framework to the designing of a practical transceiver. The goal of this project is to advance the technology readiness level of PASS for commercial adoption. In particular, these are the project objectives: Characterize the communication theoretic fundamentals of PASS; Design spectrally efficient and low-overhead channel estimation algorithms for PASS; Develop sophisticated transceiver designs to realize the full potential of PASS; Investigate two crucial applications of PASS, namely NOMA and ISAC. The project is led by the University of Manchester, and involves the University of Essex and three industrial partners. The post holder will work collaboratively with project partners to deliver high‑quality research on PASS and ensure the successful completion of all project objectives. The University actively fosters a culture of inclusion and diversity and seeks to achieve true equality of opportunity for all members of its community. The Faculty welcomes applications from all sections of the community and are committed to having a representative workforce. Across the Schools we hold Bronze and Silver Athena SWAN Awards, which recognise our commitment to equality, diversity and inclusion and particularly the advancement of women’s careers in STEM. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Professor Daniel So Email: d.so@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46153": {
      "title": "ICNS Research Associate in Neuromorphic Vision Systems",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for ICNS Research Associate in Neuromorphic Vision Systems Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results ICNS Research Associate in Neuromorphic Vision Systems Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032494 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Computer Science Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 20/10/2026 Contract Duration: 24 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Job Description The Postdoctoral Research Associate (Neuromorphic Vision Systems) plays a pivotal role in designing and implementing neuromorphic vision systems, spanning the theory of pixel operation, optimisation of processing algorithms, and hardware implementation. This position involves close collaboration with interdisciplinary teams across neuroscience, engineering, and computer science to develop functional models capable of learning, storing, and detecting patterns. The role also includes contributing to demonstrator applications that showcase the practical impact of the research. Beyond technical development, the Research Associate is expected to actively contribute to the University’s research output through data analysis, publication, and dissemination via seminars, conferences, and public engagement. Additionally, the role supports the identification and development of new research directions and the preparation of proposals for both independent and collaborative projects. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Andre Van Schaik Email: andre.vanschaik@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46188": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research associate (neuroscience)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Postdoctoral Research associate (neuroscience) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Postdoctoral Research associate (neuroscience) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032529 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £37,694 Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Immunology, Immunity to Infection, and Respiratory Medicine Hours Per Week: Full time (1FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 03/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 18 months School/Directorate: School of Biological Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking an experienced/senior postdoctoral researcher in neuroscience to explore how the immune system shapes hypothalamic circuits involved in sleep-wake cycle regulations during development and in response to inflammation. This is an exciting opportunity for a highly ambitious, competitive, and motivated individual interested in developing an innovative research programme combining immunology and neuroscience. The post holder will investigate the mechanisms involved in sculpting inhibitory and excitatory circuits in the hypothalamus, from early life through to adulthood. The successful candidate will join the Quintana Lab, based within the Lydia Becker Institute of Immunology and Inflammation and the Geoffrey Jefferson Centre for Brain Research, working within a dynamic, collaborative and inclusive research environment. You will be responsible for: To conduct original research exploring the immunological basis of hypothalamic maturation Strong abilities to conduct surgeries and behavioural studies in mice To identify and develop suitable techniques, and apparatus, for the collection and analysis of data To draft publications and prepare them for submission to refereed journals To lead on and contribute to writing bids for research grants We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Hold a PhD in neuroscience, behavioural sciences, sleep, or related disciplines. Demonstrable experience through high-impact scientific outputs as first and/or corresponding author. Extensive and up-to-date theoretical and practical knowledge in neuroscience, electrophysiology, behaviour, sleep, in vivo genetic manipulations, and related skills. Proven ability to lead project and teams as demonstrated by publications as first-authors and/or demonstrable deliverables within an industry setting. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills and ability to work with colleagues at all levels. Desirable Criteria: Experience with patent applications. Experience working in industrial R&D. Demonstrable track-record of high impact outputs as first author. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr. Juan F. Quintana Email Address: juan.quintana@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46187": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research associate",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Postdoctoral Research associate Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Postdoctoral Research associate Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032528 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £39,906 Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Immunology, Immunity to Infection, and Respiratory Medicine Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 03/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 9 months School/Directorate: School of Biological Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking an experienced postdoctoral research associate to lead an ongoing clinical study exploring the immunological basis of sleep disorders. This is an exciting opportunity for someone interested in developing translational research whilst also maintaining their ability to conduct basic research in neuroimmunology. The postholder will work collaboratively with partners at the NHS and industrial collaborators to develop this research. The role is based within the Quintana Lab, part of the Lydia Becker Institute of Immunology and Inflammation and the Geoffrey Jefferson Centre for Brain Research. The successful candidate will benefit from a rich network of national and international collaborators, ranging from basic scientists to clinical partners, within a research environment that values curiosity, passion, inclusion, humility and teamwork. You will be responsible for: To conduct original research exploring the immunological basis of sleep disorders To identify and develop suitable techniques, and apparatus, for the collection and analysis of data To draft publications and prepare them for submission to refereed journals To submit publications to refereed journals To lead on and contribute to writing bids for research grants We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Hold a PhD in immunology (or related fields) Extensive and demonstrable experience in B cell biology through first-author publications Extensive and demonstrable industrial experience in Biosciences and/or industrial R&D Extensive and up-to-date theoretical and practical knowledge in immunoparasitology Excellent technical skills in immunoparasitology and in vivo models of infection Proven ability to lead project and teams as demonstrated by publications as first-author and/or demonstrable deliverables within an industry setting Desirable Criteria: Experience working with African trypanosomes in mice at PhD level Experience working in industrial R&D Demonstrable track-record of high impact outputs as first author We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr. Juan F. Quintana Email Address: juan.quintana@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45955": {
      "title": "AVID Research and Business Engagement (RBE) Officer",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for AVID Research and Business Engagement (RBE) Officer Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results AVID Research and Business Engagement (RBE) Officer Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032296 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Materials Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 19/10/2026 Contract Duration: until 31 March 2030 School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The purpose of the role is to support businesses in the Greater Manchester region and beyond to accelerate innovation in advanced materials systems. The RBE Officer will play a crucial role in engaging businesses on projects, both in the provision of advice and in managing the transition to assessment and through translating innovation and connecting regional businesses to national supply chain needs. Key advanced materials themes include, but are not limited to advanced fibres, sustainable polymers, digital manufacturing and materials for the hydrogen economy. The role will have considerable focus also on supporting the operational delivery of the Sustainable Materials Manufacturing Centre (SMMC), engaging prospective tenants and users, coordinating visits and onboarding activities, and developing a pipeline of businesses that can access SMMC facilities, expertise and innovation programmes to support innovation and growth across the Atom Valley cluster. The post holder will work closely with the wider University research Institutes and centres (i.e. Royce/GEIC/Sustainable Materials Innovation Hub) and consortium partners’ technical and research teams to support businesses primarily SMEs throughout their journey. The RBE Officer will be line managed by the Business Development Manager at the Henry Royce Institute and will report also to the Principal Investigator on the grant. The role will be primarily based at the Sustainable Materials and Manufacturing Centre (SMMC), currently under construction at the Atom Valley Innovation District in Rochdale, with occasional travel to the UoM campus and partner sites as appropriate. Until the opening of the SMMC in mid-2027, the role will be based at the UoM campus. This is an exciting opportunity to play a key support role at the intersection of academia, industry and local government, helping to accelerate innovation and enable inclusive economic growth. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Michelle Ledward, Business Development Manager or Jill Brown, Programme Manager Email: Michelle.ledward@manchester.ac.uk or jill.brown@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support : 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Supporting Documents save_alt SAE-032296 - AVID Research and Business Engagement (RBE) Officer FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46119": {
      "title": "Vice-Dean and Head of the School&#39;s: Medical Sciences &amp; Health Sciences",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Vice-Dean and Head of the School&#39;s: Medical Sciences &amp; Health Sciences Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Vice-Dean and Head of the School's: Medical Sciences & Health Sciences Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032454 Salary: Competitive Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Manchester Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Not specified Hours Per Week: FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 15/11/2026 Contract Duration: Initial five year term, with the possibility of extension School/Directorate: Multiple Schools Job Description Help shape the future of health, discovery and education at Manchester. Two Schools. Two leadership opportunities. Vice-Dean and Head of the School of Medical Sciences Vice-Dean and Head of the School of Health Sciences The University of Manchester is seeking two outstanding academic leaders to join the Faculty of Biology, Medicine and Health (FBMH) as Vice-Dean and Head of School, leading either the School of Medical Sciences or the School of Health Sciences. These are significant leadership opportunities at a pivotal moment for our university, our faculty and the wider health and higher education sectors. As one of the UK's largest and most comprehensive faculties of its kind, FBMH brings together world leading research, outstanding education and powerful partnerships across healthcare, industry and communities. Through Manchester 2035, we are advancing knowledge, improving lives and delivering meaningful social, economic and environmental impact through research, education and innovation. Lead with purpose As Vice-Dean and Head of School, you will provide visible, inclusive and values-led leadership to your School, setting strategic direction and creating the conditions for colleagues and students to thrive. You will lead your School's contribution to the ambitions of FBMH and the University, driving excellence in research, education, innovation, knowledge exchange, public engagement and social responsibility. Working across disciplines and organisational boundaries, you will help shape solutions to some of the most pressing health and societal challenges of our time. As a member of the Faculty Executive and the University's extended leadership community, you will influence institutional strategy and decision making. Working closely with the Dean, Faculty colleagues and the Director of School Operations, you will lead change, align priorities and build a sustainable future for your School. You will also strengthen relationships across the NHS, Greater Manchester's health and care system, industry, government, civic organisations and academic partners, creating new opportunities for collaboration, innovation and impact. What you will bring You will be an accomplished academic leader with a strong track record of achievement and experience of leading within a complex research, education or healthcare environment. Academic distinction and credibility. Strategic vision and the ability to translate ambition into delivery. Experience of leading change and developing inclusive, high-performing cultures. Strong financial, operational and organisational leadership capability. The ability to inspire, engage and develop colleagues and students. A collaborative approach and commitment to partnership working. Above all, you will be a leader who builds trust, enables others to succeed and brings people together behind a shared ambition. Join Manchester. Shape what comes next. These appointments offer a rare opportunity to lead ambitious Schools within a globally recognised Faculty and to help shape the future of health, discovery and education at one of the world's leading universities. If you are inspired by the opportunity to make a lasting difference through academic leadership, we would welcome your application. Please note: These are separate appointments for the School of Medical Sciences and the School of Health Sciences. Please indicate in your cover letter if you would like to be considered for a specific School or for both. A shared Candidate Brochure applies and can be found below, with candidates assessed in the context of the School they would lead. Panel interviews are due to take place on Monday 30th November. Candidate Brochure - UoM FBMH Vice-Dean & Head of Schools Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Rosie Harmshaw Email Address: talent.attraction@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46157": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Mixed-Signal Chip Design",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Postdoctoral Research Associate in Mixed-Signal Chip Design Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Postdoctoral Research Associate in Mixed-Signal Chip Design Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032497 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 20/10/2026 Contract Duration: 30 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Job Description The University of Manchester is inviting applications for a Post-Doctoral Research Associate to lead the design of next-generation vision sensors. This role focuses on developing mixed-signal integrated circuits (ICs) for advanced imaging and edge computing applications, exploring novel approaches to integrating sensing and pixel-level processing. The research involves full-custom chip design using state-of-the-art EDA tools and fabrication of analogue and mixed-signal circuits. You will explore new transistor-level circuits through simulations and layout design, while also contributing to the back-end physical implementation of digital subsystems. The work addresses practical aspects of system implementation, including chip tape-out, post-silicon laboratory bring-up, system integration, and building practical technology demonstrations of the fabricated silicon. This position is embedded within a long-standing research programme on vision sensors and neuromorphic computing circuits in the laboratory of Prof Piotr Dudek at the University of Manchester. The role is directly associated with the EPSRC-funded project on \"On-sensor Computer Vision,\" carried out in collaboration with Imperial College London, University of Bristol, and industrial partners. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension, 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure, Ride to work and EV car scheme available. For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Piotr Dudek Email Address: p.dudek@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46072": {
      "title": "Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator (Student Support and Wellbeing)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator (Student Support and Wellbeing) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator (Student Support and Wellbeing) Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032417 Salary: £33,002 to £36,636 pro rata per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: School of Arts, Languages and Cultures Administration Hours Per Week: 0.5 FTE  17.5 hrs per week Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 26/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Arts, Languages and Cultures Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Teaching, Learning Student Experience Coordinator (Student Support and Wellbeing). We are looking for a Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator to join our Student Support and Wellbeing team within the School of Arts, Languages and Cultures. This is an excellent opportunity for an organised, proactive and student focused professional to play a key role in supporting student wellbeing, academic engagement and success. You will coordinate a team delivering frontline support services and work closely with students, academic colleagues and specialist support services to ensure students receive effective and timely support. About the role Working in a busy and rewarding environment, you will: Lead and coordinate a team providing student support and wellbeing services. Support students with a range of issues, ensuring they are signposted to appropriate University support services. Coordinate key student support processes, including mitigating circumstances, fitness to study and student engagement monitoring. Act as Disability Coordinator for the School, working closely with the Disability Advisory and Support Service (DASS). Develop effective working relationships with academic and professional services colleagues across the University. Use data and insight to support service delivery and continuous improvement. Contribute to a culture of excellent customer service, innovation and student centred practice. About you We are looking for someone with: Experience of working in student support, student administration, higher education or a comparable customer-focused environment. Experience of supervising or coordinating the work of others. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build positive relationships with a wide range of stakeholders. Strong organisational skills and the ability to manage competing priorities in a complex environment. A compassionate and professional approach when dealing with sensitive student matters. A commitment to delivering excellent service and improving the student experience. Strong digital skills and confidence working with a range of systems and data. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Sarah Thompson Email: sarah.thompson-2@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46096": {
      "title": "Senior Technician (Mechatronics)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Technician (Mechatronics) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Technician (Mechatronics) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032476 Salary: £33,002 to £36,636 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 Hours Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 26/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description This role offers an exciting opportunity to play a key part in supporting Undergraduate students in the area of mechatronics. within the Faculty of Science and Engineering. Working as a Senior Technician within technical platform, you will apply your technical expertise to support practical student projects and student society activities. Through design and development you will help create the facilities, equipment and technical solutions that enable discovery, learning and innovation. Your contribution will directly enhance the student experience, support academic excellence, and help deliver sector leading teaching support. As Technical Staff, you are part of Professional Services. Together, all technical staff who work within the Faculty of Science and Engineering (FSE) form FSE Technical Operations. FSE Technical Operations is organised into five key functional areas: • Research • Teaching • Infrastructure & Facilities • Workshops • Computing, Robotics & AI Teaching - The teaching technical team provides high quality technical advice and support to students of all years of education. The team are led by Technical Specialists (TTS) who are responsible for managing and coordinating technical support for teaching in each of their specific disciplines. This includes all related teaching space, associated equipment and ensuring all Health and Safety obligations are met. The team support a host of taught units within each department. These can be based in computer clusters, bespoke labs or other shared areas.. The team works closely with Timetabling, Discipline Heads of Education and laboratory tutors to advise on experiment design and the technical resource required for its delivery. The role will be part of the Makerspace team. The team is made up of 3 dedicated technicians and is supported by technical teams from other areas. The team support 3rd and 4th year group and individual projects and societies including Robosoc, Giant foamboard aircraft, UAV and ManSEDS. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: John Bramwell Email: John.Bramwell@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46140": {
      "title": "Senior Technician (EEE (Teaching))",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Technician (EEE (Teaching)) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Technician (EEE (Teaching)) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032473 Salary: £33,002 to £36,636 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 30/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Senior Teaching Technician will provide specialist technical support for practical teaching across Electrical and Electronic Engineering, working collaboratively with technical and academic colleagues to prepare, maintain and improve laboratories, equipment and learning activities. Through expert guidance, problem-solving and the promotion of safe and effective working practices, the role will help students build practical engineering skills and confidence. This is an opportunity to make a visible difference to the quality, reliability and inclusivity of the student learning experience while contributing to the continued development of high-quality teaching facilities. As Technical Staff, you are part of Professional Services. Together, all technical staff who work within the Faculty of Science and Engineering (FSE) form FSE Technical Operations. FSE Technical Operations is organised into five key functional areas: Research Teaching Infrastructure & Facilities Workshops Computing, Robotics & AI Within FSE Technical Operations, you will belong to the Teaching area. Teaching - The teaching technical team provides high-quality technical advice and support to students of all years of education. They also support academic staff in the diverse range of areas within the Faculty of Science and Engineering. The team are led by several Teaching Technical Specialists (TTS) who are responsible for managing and coordinating technical support for teaching in each of their specific disciplines. This includes all related teaching space, associated equipment and ensuring all Health and Safety obligations are met. The team support a host of taught units within each department. These can be based in computer clusters, bespoke labs or other shared areas. They also support student projects, student societies and field trips in the areas of Civil engineering, Aero and Earth Science. The team works closely with Timetabling, Discipline Heads of Education and laboratory tutors to advise on experiment design and the technical resource required for its delivery. Within the Teaching area, you will be part of the Department of Electronic and Electrical Engineering The EEE Teaching Technical Team is a collaborative team of six technicians who provide specialist technical support for undergraduate practical teaching, student projects, academic staff and research activities across Electrical and Electronic Engineering. Reporting to and working closely with the Teaching Technical Specialist, the team plays a key role in the delivery of high-quality teaching and research by preparing, maintaining and developing laboratory equipment, instrumentation, components and teaching facilities. The team supports students during practical activities and project work, provides technical guidance to academics and researchers, and contributes to the safe, effective and efficient operation of teaching and research environments. Collectively, the team brings expertise in electronics, printed circuit board design, construction and repair, microcontrollers and embedded systems, fault-finding, equipment maintenance and safe laboratory practice. Responsibilities include technical project support, diagnostic analysis, equipment management, production of bespoke project hardware, and the provision of specialist training and guidance to students and staff. The team also supports department specific activities including PCB manufacture and repair, embedded systems projects, technical student societies and engineering design competitions. The successful candidate will join an experienced and supportive team committed to sharing knowledge, continuously improving facilities and processes, and enhancing the quality, safety and reliability of the practical learning environment. Through supporting laboratory-based teaching, undergraduate design projects and research activities, the team helps students develop the practical engineering skills required for academic and professional success. As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Kerry Plant Email: kerry.plant@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46084": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Materials Chemistry",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Materials Chemistry Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Materials Chemistry Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032427 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Chemistry Hours Per Week: Full time (1FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 31/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 15 months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Associate within the Department of Chemistry, School of Natural Sciences. This exciting research project aims to produce ultra-thin polymer-based membranes and fluidic chips with polymers of intrinsic porosity (PIMs), to study molecular and ionic permeation for ionic gating, ionic memory applications, and molecular separations. You will be expected to publish academic papers and contribute towards advanced new concepts. You will be responsible for: Conduct original research and collaborate within the group and internationally as per the project needs Develop research objectives and proposals for own or joint research, with the assistance of a mentor if required. Conduct individual and collaborative research projects. Write up research work for publication. Training students and others in skills appropriate to the project. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria A PhD (or be about to obtain) in electrochemistry, polymer science or membranes with experimental work in chemistry, materials science or chemical engineering. A strong publication record in internationally peer-reviewed journals and an ability to write scientific reports and contribute to writing research papers. Experience of working with electrochemical measurements, electrical source meters, precision electrical meters, patch-clamp amplifiers, neuromorphic measurements. Significant experience of nanofabrication, measurement and characterization of ultrathin membranes, 2D materials van der Waals heterostructures. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. Desirable Criteria Expertise in electrochemistry. Experience of working in nanofluidics/membrane technologies. Experience in clean room micro/nanofabrication and working with 2D materials devices. Experience of sequencing measurements with biomolecules. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see the University of Manchester Benefits page. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working arrangements. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr Ashok Keerthi or Prof Radha Boya Email: ashok.keerthi@manchester.ac.uk or radha.boya@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support People.Recruitment@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46124": {
      "title": "Project Manager",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Project Manager Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Project Manager Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032459 Salary: £47,389 - £58,225 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Projects Team Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 22/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate of Estates and Facilities Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Role: As a Project Manager within the Minor Works Team, you will lead the delivery of a varied portfolio of capital projects across the University estate, taking responsibility from initial planning and development through to successful completion. Acting as the University’s client representative, you will manage multiple projects and workstreams, coordinating consultants, contractors, stakeholders, priorities and risks to ensure delivery against agreed scope, programme, budget and quality requirements. You will work closely with colleagues and stakeholders across the University, providing effective project leadership, maintaining strong project controls and ensuring emerging risks and issues are managed or escalated appropriately through established governance arrangements. Your Responsibilities Will Include: Act as the University’s client representative, leading assigned capital projects from feasibility and planning through to successful completion and handover. Manage multiple projects and workstreams, coordinating priorities, dependencies, risks, programme and budget to achieve agreed outcomes. Maintain effective project governance and controls, including programmes, budgets, forecasts, risk registers, change control and performance reporting. Lead and coordinate consultants and contractors, supporting procurement and appointments and actively managing external team performance throughout delivery. Build effective relationships with project sponsors and stakeholders, providing clear advice on delivery options, risks, programme and budget and escalating key issues where required. Ensure projects are delivered safely, compliantly and sustainably, meeting relevant legislation, CDM and Building Regulations, University requirements and sustainability objectives. About You: Degree and/or relevant professional qualification in construction, engineering, surveying, architecture or project management. Experience managing capital, infrastructure, refurbishment or construction projects within a complex organisational environment. Experience managing multiple concurrent projects or workstreams, coordinating priorities, dependencies, risks and delivery activity. Strong understanding of project management and delivery, including procurement, contracts, budgets, forecasting, risk, governance and reporting. Strong stakeholder management, communication and influencing skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and work effectively with internal and external stakeholders. What You’ll Get: 29 days annual leave, plus bank holidays and December closure days Generous pension scheme Flexible and hybrid working Access to a wide range of staff benefits, including discounts, wellbeing support, and development programmes Explore full benefits: www.manchester.ac.uk/connect/jobs/benefits-working-here For informal enquiries please contact the Talent Attraction Team talent.attraction@manchester.ac.uk Supporting Documents save_alt Project Manager Job Description (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46123": {
      "title": "Senior Project Manager",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Project Manager Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Project Manager Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032458 Salary: £59,966 - £71,566 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Projects Team Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 22/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate of Estates and Facilities Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Role: As a senior member of the Estates and Facilities Major Projects team, you will lead the delivery of complex, high value and strategically important capital projects and programmes across the University estate. Working with a high degree of autonomy, you will take projects from planning through to completion, managing multiple priorities, risks, budgets and stakeholders to ensure successful delivery against time, cost, quality, safety and sustainability objectives. You will act as a trusted adviser to senior stakeholders, providing expert professional and commercial advice, managing consultants and contractors, and resolving complex delivery challenges. You will also play a key role in strengthening project and programme governance, controls and ways of working across Major Projects, helping ensure the University’s capital investment delivers long term value. Your Responsibilities Will Include: Provide autonomous senior client side leadership for complex, high value and strategically important capital projects and programmes, from initiation and brief development through delivery, handover and operational use. Take ownership of multiple concurrent projects and programmes, managing interdependencies, risks, resources, approvals, constraints and stakeholder impacts to achieve agreed scope, time, cost, quality and safety outcomes. Own robust financial and project controls, including significant budgets, cashflow forecasts, schedules, risk registers, change control and performance reporting, providing clear delivery confidence to senior stakeholders. Provide authoritative commercial and professional leadership, shaping procurement and contracting strategies and managing consultant and contractor appointments, performance, cost risk, change and claims to secure value for money. Lead senior stakeholder engagement across academic, professional services and operational areas, managing competing priorities and translating complex technical, commercial and programme information into clear recommendations and decisions. Provide professional leadership to multidisciplinary teams, consultants and contractors, setting performance standards, challenging underperformance and intervening where delivery is off track to agree and implement recovery actions. About You: Substantial experience of leading complex, high value and strategically important capital projects and programmes within a large, multi stakeholder organisation. Proven ability to manage standalone projects and programmes of multiple interdependent workstreams, including planning, dependencies, risks, governance, reporting and delivery to agreed outcomes. Demonstrable experience operating with a high degree of autonomy and professional judgement, advising senior stakeholders and governance forums on complex project, programme, commercial and delivery matters. Excellent knowledge of construction procurement, contract administration and commercial management, including NEC contracts and effective management of consultant and contractor performance. Highly developed stakeholder management, influencing, analytical and communication skills, with the ability to interpret complex information, manage competing priorities and provide clear recommendations in high risk or uncertain situations. What You Will Get: 29 days annual leave, plus bank holidays and December closure days Generous pension scheme Flexible and hybrid working Access to a wide range of staff benefits, including discounts, wellbeing support, and development programmes Explore full benefits: www.manchester.ac.uk/connect/jobs/benefits-working-here For informal enquiries please contact the Talent Attraction Team talent.attraction@manchester.ac.uk Supporting Documents save_alt Senior Project Manager Job Description (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46080": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Cardiometabolic Health",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Cardiometabolic Health Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Cardiometabolic Health Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032501 Salary: £37,694 - £41,064 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Developmental Biology and Medicine Hours Per Week: Full time (1  FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 15/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 18 months School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Research Associate in Cardiometabolic Health We are seeking a motivated and collaborative Research Associate in Cardiometabolic Health to join the NIHR-funded Maternity Disparities Consortium. You will support impactful research and knowledge mobilisation within the pregnancy, birth and early postnatal maternal health theme, with a particular focus on cardiometabolic health and care outcomes. You will be responsible for: To work collaboratively with colleagues from Sheffield Hallam University and Newcastle University, supporting others in a flexible manner To conduct participatory system mapping using stakeholder and PPI workshops to build a systems overview of existing cardiometabolic care pathways, gaps, intersectional inequalities, and potential barriers and facilitators to a cardiometabolic care bundle; to identify key actors critical to the project’s successful implementation To build on existing digital referral systems at Manchester in the postnatal period following a diagnosis of gestational diabetes To explore the wider/intersectional drivers of cardiometabolic inequalities and how these may act as barriers to following evidence based behavioural interventions, building on behavioural research at Sheffield and Manchester To collaborate with Birmingham to build on digital prototype research of postnatal home blood pressure monitoring We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria Have, or be about to obtain, a PhD in behavioural, implementation science or other related health fields Experience of intervention development and/or implementation science research Experience of qualitative research including co-production/participatory approaches Experience of active engagement with community groups and stakeholders Ability to work as a member of a multidisciplinary team, across insitutions, and to develop and maintain good relationships with colleagues and healthcare professionals Desirable Criteria Experience or an interest in patient and public involvement and engagement in research (PPIE) Experience of conducting research in NHS settings Experience of maternity-related research Experience of cardiometabolic health research We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Samina Faisal Email Address: samina.faisal@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46144": {
      "title": "Junior Sous Chef - Central Production Kitchen",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Junior Sous Chef - Central Production Kitchen Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Junior Sous Chef - Central Production Kitchen Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032479 Salary: £31,408 per annum Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Hospitality and Events Hours Per Week: 40 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 21/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate of Estates and Facilities Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Overall Purpose Working under the direction of the Sous Chef, the postholder will support the preparation, production, packing and dispatch of consistently high-quality food for Hospitality services, events and outlets. The role combines skilled food production with the day-to-day coordination of colleagues on shift, translating production plans, recipes and service requirements into safe, efficient and timely kitchen activity while promoting high standards, teamwork and continuous improvement. The postholder will coordinate an allocated section or production activity, check product quality and consistency, and help ensure food is cooled, stored, labelled, packed and dispatched safely and on time. They will also support colleagues and deputise for the Sous Chef on agreed operational matters. About the Team The role is based within the Hospitality Central Production team at the Central Production Unit, Oxford Road, Manchester. The team produces food for catered conferences and events, lunches, dinners, banqueting, weddings, cafés and other food outlets across the University. The operation is focused on food safety, service excellence, teamwork, sustainability and the consistent delivery of high-quality food. Catering at the University encompasses a broad and diverse range of business areas – including retail outlets, catered halls, student bars, conferencing, events and hospitality. There are many benefits to working at the University: • 26 days holiday plus Bank Holidays • Generous pension scheme with generous employer contribution • A strong commitment to personal and career development, as well as wellbeing and mental health support • Annual salary review To find out more about staff benefits and working within catering at the University, visit our catering staff website: www.umc-limited.co.uk We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Eddie MaKuwaza – Executive Head Chef Email Address: eddie.makuwaza@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032479 Junior Sous Chef - Central Production Kitchen FP's (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46176": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032518 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 15/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 12 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative Research Associate to develop a first-principles benchmark framework for analysing and optimising power quality and disturbance rejection on the more electric aircraft DC bus. Working with an industry partner, you will take the framework from analytical concept through to a hardware-validated methodology. You will be responsible for: Benchmark framework development – extend and refine the power-frequency capability envelope method (elicitation of maximum sustainable power per frequency band under energy, power, and converter constraints), and the coverage/margin metrics used to assess power quality against mission demand. Model generation methodology – develop a methodology for generating reduced-order or behavioural models of the DC bus system directly from time-domain simulation of converter models, so that the frequency-domain capability envelopes can be produced systematically from simulated converter and energy-storage dynamics rather than bespoke derivation for each case. Benchmarking methodology – formalise the procedure for benchmarking real or simulated DC bus behaviour against the capability envelopes and mission demand spectrum, including how margin, coverage, and disturbance containment are quantified and reported. Hardware development and validation – set up and test a bidirectional converter that can inject harmonic content as a hardware demonstrator, and use it to validate the generated models and benchmarking methodology against measured hardware behaviour. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria A PhD (or thesis submitted) in Electrical/Electronic Engineering, Power Electronics, or a closely related discipline. A strong background in power electronic converter analysis, modelling, and design, including DC–DC converter topologies and control. Proficiency in time-domain circuit/system simulation (e.g. LTspice, PLECS, MATLAB/Simulink, or PSIM) and in deriving models or reduced-order representations from simulation data. Familiarity with frequency-domain analysis and signal processing (e.g. FFT, spectral decomposition) as applied to power systems. Good written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to report technical work clearly to an industry sponsor, and the ability to work both independently and collaboratively within a research group. Desirable Criteria Hands-on power electronics hardware experience: converter/PCB design, build, and test in a lab environment; experience with bidirectional converters and/or supercapacitor or battery-interfaced converters is a strong advantage. Experience with energy storage systems (batteries and/or supercapacitors), including their power-energy trade-offs, and with hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) or real-time simulation platforms for power electronic systems. Familiarity with more electric aircraft (MEA) power system architectures and relevant aerospace standards. Experience with data acquisition, instrumentation, and test automation for power hardware testing. A track record of disseminating research through peer-reviewed journal or conference publications. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews Name: Cheng Zhang Email Address: cheng.zhang@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46177": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Electromagnetic Non-Destructive Evaluation and Inspection",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Electromagnetic Non-Destructive Evaluation and Inspection Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Electromagnetic Non-Destructive Evaluation and Inspection Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032517 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 15/10/2026 Contract Duration: 2 years 6 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Associate in Electromagnetic Non-Destructive Evaluation and Inspection. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to the EPSRC-funded Frontiers in Electromagnetic Non-Destructive Evaluation Research (FENDER) programme, which seeks to bring game-changing ideas and exploit advances in complimentary areas of science to trigger a UK-led renaissance in electromagnetic NDE, boosting the UK’s reputation, energising a world-leading research community and achieving downstream commercial impact via a broad network of industrial partners. The Programme is led by the University of Manchester and involves four other Universities (Bristol, Warwick, Newcastle and Strathclyde). The initiative is supported by approximately 25 industrial partners which are some of the major NDE users, within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will have responsibility to: Model and optimise electromagnetic sensors and coil configurations. Design, prototype and validate EM sensors and arrays for defects and materials. Develop sensor-interface electronics and multi-channel data acquisition, improving the real time hardware and firmware, and implementing algorithms with other team members. Develop real-time acquisition, processing and control software. Assist in the development of the data acquisition system for sensor arrays Support commercialisation partners in the further development of systems into a commercial product We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Possess, or be about to obtain, a PhD in a relevant STEM discipline or have equivalent industrial research experience. Proven track record of successfully applying electromagnetic theory to engineering applications. Demonstrate a record of publications with leading academic journals and be able to write scientific reports and research papers Be able to efficiently present research findings at national and international meetings and conferences Possess excellent oral and written scientific communication skills We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Professor Wuliang Yin Email Address: Wuliang.yin@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45983": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Ethereum",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Ethereum Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Ethereum Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032448 Salary: £37,694-£46,049 Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Computer Science Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 15/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 6 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Associate in Ethereum . This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to research within a dynamic and inclusive environment. The post holder will design and build AI agents and verification engines for the formal verification and automated testing of Ethereum consensus specifications, as part of the project “Bounded Model Checking for Verifying and Testing Ethereum Consensus Specifications”. The successful candidate will join the Department of Computer Science within the School of Engineering, working in the Systems and Software Security (S3) Research Group led by Dr. Lucas Cordeiro. You will be responsible for: Extending ESBMC’s Python frontend to model check the Ethereum consensus specification’s reference implementation, including the fork choice component Designing new bounded model checking techniques for verifying safety and reachability properties without manual abstraction of the specification Building AI agents that translate specification clauses into formal properties and drive the verification engine’s search and triage of counterexamples Developing agentic workflows that use the verification engine to generate test suites traceable to individual specification clauses Writing scientific articles on the verification engine, agent design, and test generation results for security and software engineering venues We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: PhD in computer science or a closely related field, in formal verification, software testing, programming languages, or AI agents Strong programming skills in Python and/or C++ Knowledge of formal verification techniques such as model checking, SAT/SMT solving, or symbolic execution Experience building or applying AI agents, including LLM-based agents, for software engineering tasks Excellent written and verbal communication skills Desirable Criteria: Experience with bounded model checkers such as ESBMC, CBMC, or similar tools Familiarity with the Ethereum consensus specification or blockchain protocols more broadly Track record of peer-reviewed publications in formal methods, software testing, or AI Experience with coverage-guided test generation or fuzzing Familiarity with Git-based version control and open-source software development We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working arrangements. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Lucas Cordeiro Email Address: lucas.cordeiro@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46143": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Indoor Air Quality",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Indoor Air Quality Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Indoor Air Quality Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032478 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Not specified Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 25/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 42 months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Associate in Indoor Air Quality . This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to the Retrofit Advanced Materials & Manufacturing Innovation Centre (RAMMIC), a major collaborative research and innovation programme supported through the UKRI Local Innovation Partnership Fund. RAMMIC brings together Greater Manchester capabilities in building performance, advanced materials, indoor air quality, acoustics, environmental sustainability assessment, and innovation support to establish a collaborative innovation ecosystem for smart and sustainable retrofit products and processes. You will be responsible for: Develop and lead research examining indoor air quality within retrofit environments at the Energy House. Design experimental protocols for evaluating retrofit products, materials, ventilation systems and other interventions. Undertake measurements in Energy House facilities and other project locations. Deploy, operate and maintain appropriate sensor systems and research-grade atmospheric instrumentation. Work closely with the Research Associate in Sustainable Materials to coordinate experiments and integrate indoor air-quality and material-sustainability evidence. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria Have, or be about to obtain, a PhD or equivalent qualification in atmospheric science, or a very closely related discipline. Specialist knowledge of atmospheric composition measurements and their application to indoor or outdoor air quality. Demonstrable experience of designing and undertaking atmospheric measurement studies. Experience of quantitative data processing, quality assurance, analysis and interpretation. Understanding of the physical and chemical processes influencing indoor air quality. Desirable Criteria Experience of indoor air-quality measurements in homes, test buildings or other occupied environments. Experience of online mass spectrometry, including Chemical Ionisation Mass Spectrometry, or other advanced gas- or particle-phase measurement techniques. Knowledge of emissions from building materials, consumer products or retrofit interventions. Experience of investigating ventilation, damp, mould related indoor air-quality problems. Experience of controlled intervention studies or building-performance experiments. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working arrangements. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Thomas Bannan Email Address: thomas.bannan@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46043": {
      "title": "RESEARCH PROJECT MANAGER – SKA / SYNCHRONISATION AND TIMING (SAT)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for RESEARCH PROJECT MANAGER – SKA / SYNCHRONISATION AND TIMING (SAT) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results RESEARCH PROJECT MANAGER – SKA / SYNCHRONISATION AND TIMING (SAT) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032384 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: School of Natural Sciences Admin Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 15/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 12 months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Project Manager – SKA / Synchronisation and Timing (SAT). Reporting to the Project Support Operations Manager the Research Project Manager will be responsible for administration of the University of Manchester’s involvement in the SKA Telescope. The Project Manager is needed to oversee key deliverables in the Synchronisation and Timing (SAT) area, amongst other things. You will be responsible for: Provide a totality of project management function: detailed work plans, milestone setting, budget control, meetings management and preparing reports. Collate, analyse, and present data on project finances, progress, and risks, producing briefings and reports, both verbal and written to funders/advisory boards/internal and external stakeholders. Working closely with SKAO to oversee and manage key industry contracts, ensuring deliverables, timelines and budgets are adhered to while resolving any issues Drive the project deadlines to ensure activity is delivered in a timely manner. Line management of Project Support staff directly on the project, providing a good quality induction, probation monitoring, P&DR, pastoral support and daily workload direction to ensure project progress. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria First degree and/or significant experience in project management/administration. Good working knowledge of SAFe Agile framework practices and procedures. Knowledge of industry contract management practices Experience of supporting management to successfully deliver large-scale projects and programmes in a responsible capacity. Excellent organisational skills with the ability to manage and prioritise conflicting tasks in a busy environment and to manage competing deadlines. Desirable Criteria Experience of working in Higher Education Institutions, particularly within astronomy or other STEM department A relevant qualification in industry contract management practices e.g., NEC training Familiarity with SKA projects Knowledge of project platforms and tools such as Confluence, ALIM, CEMAR etc Experience of STEM communication, e.g., writing articles, social media updates etc to communicate project aims and progress We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Claire Huyton claire.huyton@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt SAE-032384 FP Research Manager (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46054": {
      "title": "RESEARCH ASSOCIATE IN SOLID-STATE NMR SPECTROSCOPY",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for RESEARCH ASSOCIATE IN SOLID-STATE NMR SPECTROSCOPY Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results RESEARCH ASSOCIATE IN SOLID-STATE NMR SPECTROSCOPY Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032399 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Chemical Engineering Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 31/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 36 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Job Description * Interviews are expected to take place at the end of November or early December. * Please submit a covering letter and CV as a merged document (must be under 1MB). We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Associate in Solid-State NMR Spectroscopy . This Postdoctoral Research Associate (PDRA) position is part of a new £7m EPSRC funded Programme Grant that brings together researchers from the Universities of Durham, Leeds and Manchester investigating the formation of Molecular Solid Solutions (MoSS). The project will develop and apply cutting-edge techniques in crystal synthesis, characterisation and modelling to generate new understanding of MoSS, with applications across pharmaceuticals, agrochemicals and food industries. Based within the Department of Chemical Engineering, you will work closely with researchers across the consortium and have opportunities to collaborate, attend network events and undertake research placements. You will be responsible for: Conduct individual and collaborative research projects according to the overall needs of the research program. Develop research objectives and proposals for own or joint research. Write up research work for publication. Continually update knowledge and understanding in field or specialism. Translate knowledge of advances in the subject area into research activity. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: A PhD in the physical sciences or related subject. Experience in implementing a wide range of advanced solid-state NMR experiments. Experience with multinuclear solid-state NMR spectroscopy (e.g. 1H, 2H, 13C, 15N, 17O, 19F and other nuclei as appropriate). Good data analysis skills, including processing and simulation of NMR data. Ability to work both independently and collaboratively as a member of a multi-disciplinary team. Desirable Criteria: Practical experience running a DNP MAS NMR spectrometer and associated experiments. Practical experience with computational modelling (e.g. spin dynamics simulations, molecular dynamics, DFT). Experience correlating NMR data with structural information derived from diffraction or computational methods. Experience in the characterisation of molecular solids and/or crystalline organic materials. Experience with complementary characterisation methods such as PXRD, DSC, TGA, microscopy, Raman, IR spectroscopy, or solid-form analysis. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr Daniel Lee Email Address: daniel.lee@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45779": {
      "title": "Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator (Programmes and Curriculum (ITET))",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator (Programmes and Curriculum (ITET)) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator (Programmes and Curriculum (ITET)) Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032182 Salary: £33,002 - £36,636 per annum (depending on relevant experience) Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: SEED Administration Hours Per Week: 35 hours Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 22/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Environment, Education & Development Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator (Teacher Education). This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute and support all our placement activities for our students in our Initial Teacher Education (ITE) programmes. The role includes managing a small team, liaising with students, academic and professional services teams, partner schools and external bodies (including the Department for Education (DfE) and Ofsted) within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: Providing line management for the Initial Teacher Education team. Coordinating the disbursal of student bursaries, travel awards, mentor and school payments and ensuring accurate records are maintained for both internal and external audits, and Ofsted inspections. Supporting the DfE census, ensuring all trainee records are included and bursary information is accurately recorded. Maintaining accurate records of students and placements are kept in line with university policies and procedures. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Ability to supervise and motivate staff. Excellent communication skills; numeracy skills (demonstrating both accuracy and attention to detail); and organisational skills. Ability to solve problems creatively, with experience of contributing to the development and improvement of systems and procedures. A flexible team player with the ability to support colleagues. Desirable Criteria: Experience of working in an ITE or education-related administrative environment. Experience of working with external regulatory or compliance bodies. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Catriona Fraser Email Address: catriona.fraser@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46171": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032510 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum,  depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Molecular and Cellular Function Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 14/10/2026 Contract Duration: 12 months School/Directorate: School of Biological Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Postdoctoral Research Associate. This role offers an exciting opportunity to develop methacrylated hyaluronic acid hydrogel systems for controlled stiffening and softening during live-cell imaging, enabling new research into mechanobiology, integrin-mediated cell–matrix interactions and mechanically regulated cell behaviour. The postholder will also help establish robust hydrogel workflows across the laboratory and develop applications for external research funding within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: Developing and optimising methacrylated hyaluronic acid hydrogel systems for controlled stiffening and softening during live-cell imaging. Characterising and validating hydrogel properties, reproducibility and compatibility with mammalian cell culture. Investigating integrin-dependent mechanobiology at cell–matrix adhesions, and preparing the findings for publication. Establishing robust hydrogel fabrication and quality-control workflows, and providing practical training and support to other members of the laboratory. Leading research outputs and developing applications for external research funding in collaboration with colleagues across relevant disciplines. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: A PhD in materials science, biomaterials, bioengineering, cell biology or a closely related discipline, together with substantial postdoctoral or equivalent research experience at the interface of biomaterials and cell biology. Extensive hands-on experience of independently developing, adapting and troubleshooting multiple materially distinct biomaterial platforms for biological research, including their application to patient-derived cancer-cell models. An established research track record in mechanobiology and experience investigating receptor-mediated mechanotransduction, including GPCR/G-protein, integrin or related cell-surface signalling systems. Extensive experimental expertise in investigating receptor-mediated signalling using complementary biochemical, cellular and imaging approaches. This should include protein functionalisation, immunoblotting, immunocytochemistry, co-immunoprecipitation, small-GTPase activity assays and cellular fractionation, together with experience of super-resolution microscopy, such as STED or an equivalent technique, and analysis of transcriptomic datasets, such as bulk RNA sequencing. Evidence of research independence and leadership appropriate to a senior postdoctoral researcher, including leading research outputs and experience developing research grant and fellowship applications. Desirable Criteria: Experience working with hyaluronic acid-based, methacrylated or other photocrosslinkable hydrogel systems, including the use of light-mediated approaches to control biomaterial fabrication or material properties. Experience applying quantitative live-cell imaging to investigate cell–material interactions or mechanically regulated cell behaviour, including the use of microfluidic devices to examine the effects of physical forces on cell behaviour and function. Experience characterising the physical or mechanical properties of biomaterials using techniques such as rheology, atomic force microscopy or mechanical testing. Experience measuring integrin activation, focal-adhesion signalling or related cell–matrix adhesion processes. Experience establishing reproducible laboratory workflows and providing practical training or technical support to other researchers. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: John Robert Davis Email Address: john-robert.davis@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45721": {
      "title": "DMEx Information Officer",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for DMEx Information Officer Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results DMEx Information Officer Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032073 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Sir Henry Royce Institute for Advanced Materials Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 14/10/2026 Contract Duration: 26 months School/Directorate: FSE Research Institutes Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Defence Materials Centre of Excellence (DMEx) is expanding as our programme continues to accelerate. We are excited to be recruiting an Information Officer to join our growing team, supporting the next phase of development and maturity of this major Dstl-funded national research centre. This new post forms part of the continued development of DMEx's operational capability as the Centre grows in scale, complexity, and ambition. As our partnerships and research activities expand, we are strengthening our approach to information governance, security compliance, assurance, and operational reporting to support the delivery of sensitive defence-related research programmes. DMEx brings together world-leading researchers, specialist engineers, defence stakeholders, and professional services experts from across academia, government, and industry. Operating within a unique and evolving defence research environment, the Centre requires robust governance, secure ways of working, and effective management of information and data. This role offers an exciting opportunity to help shape and support those arrangements within a dynamic and high-impact organisation. As the DMEx Information Officer, you will provide operational support across information governance, data assurance, security compliance, reporting, and continuous improvement activities. You will work closely with the Operations Manager, Information Governance specialists, Professional Services colleagues, and external partners to ensure that appropriate controls, processes, and assurance mechanisms are embedded across the Centre's activities. Typical responsibilities will include maintaining compliance and assurance records, supporting audits and investigations, coordinating governance reporting, monitoring access controls and permissions, supporting information governance activities, maintaining data quality across operational systems, and contributing to the development of processes, guidance, and reporting dashboards. You will also play an important role in promoting a culture of secure and responsible information management across DMEx. Based at the Royce Institute with hybrid working (typically two days onsite), you will join a supportive, collaborative, and fast-paced team that is genuinely enthusiastic about delivering world-class research and innovation in support of UK defence and security objectives. Successful candidates may be required to undertake BPSS and potentially Security Clearance. Due to the nature of this clearance, candidates must have sole UK nationality. DMEx has an ethnically diverse team of UK nationals. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Len Williamson Email: len.williamson@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Supporting Documents save_alt SAE-032073 DMEX Information Officer JD (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45951": {
      "title": "R&amp;I Projects Team Administrator",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for R&amp;I Projects Team Administrator Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results R&I Projects Team Administrator Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032293 Salary: £29,588 - £32,080  per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Faculty Office - BMH - Administration Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 21/10/2026 Contract Duration: N/A School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Overall Purpose The Faculty and University of Manchester regard the development and implementation of an effective research strategy for Biology, Medicine and Health as central and crucial to its continuing success. This post is held within the Research & Innovation Projects Team. The team provides a professional service to support the development and implementation of the Faculty’s research and innovation strategy. It works closely with the academic community to deliver this strategy. About the Team The Research & Innovation Projects Team supports the Faculty by: • Coordinating FBMH internationalisation teaching and research partnerships and collaboration opportunities • Running the Faculty Fellowship Academy, helping researchers gain externally-funded fellowships, from doctoral studies to senior levels • Working closely alongside the Faculty Research Support Services team, which provides operational support for administrative and financial aspects of research grant management. • Supporting the formation and ongoing management of research networks by coordinating meetings, workshops and large events that help collaborations grow and prosper. This is in addition to the planning, coordination and management of projects and initiatives • Providing strategic and administrative support to the Vice Dean for Research and Innovation, Head of Research and Business Engagement and the Research Development and Innovation Manager Our benefits include: (Grades 1-5) Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Annette Barber Email Address: annette.barber@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-032293 R&I Projects Team Administrator FP's (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46049": {
      "title": "Technical Specialist (High Voltage)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Technical Specialist (High Voltage) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Technical Specialist (High Voltage) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032416 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 21/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Technical Specialist (High Voltage) enables world-class research and teaching in high-voltage engineering. Through the provision of specialist technical expertise and support within the high-voltage Platform, the role helps create an environment where complex experimental activities can be undertaken effectively and with confidence. By supporting the development of new knowledge, technologies and research capabilities, the post holder contributes to the University's research excellence, student experience, innovation ambitions and wider societal impact. • Support the work of the high-voltage Platform within the Faculty of Science and Engineering. The primary focus of this role will be providing support for the development and delivery of our activities in high-voltage engineering. • Be expected to play a key role in ensuring our experimental facilities run efficiently. They will be expected to have a close and supportive working relationship with the platform lead, academic staff, and researchers de-livering research projects. • Will need to collaborate, cooperate and work effectively with other platforms utilising facilities and Plat-forms such as Mechanical Testing, Optical and Electronic, Advanced Manufacturing, Workshops and Surface via the PPMS booking system. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Ramy Afia – Senior Technical Specialist Email Address: ramy.afia@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk T echnical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46062": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032413 Salary: £37,694 - £41,064 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Cardiovascular Sciences Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 20/10/2026 Contract Duration: 36 Months School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description This is an outstanding opportunity for a Postdoctoral Research Associate with a background in cardiovascular biology, cell biology, molecular biology, mitochondrial biology or a related discipline to work within the Kitmitto Research Group under the supervision of Professor Ashraf Kitmitto. The post holder will be part of a multidisciplinary research team based within the Division of Cardiovascular Sciences at The University of Manchester. The successful candidate will support the aims of a British Heart Foundation-funded research project grant to advance our work showing that mitochondrial quality control pathways are impaired during the early stages of type 2 diabetes. This innovative project will define the molecular mechanisms leading to defects in mitochondrial structure-function and the development of diabetic heart failure, evaluate novel therapeutic strategies, and investigate potential biomarkers and molecular signatures for patient stratification. This position offers an ideal opportunity for an enthusiastic and highly motivated scientist to work within a collaborative and interdisciplinary environment, also working with Professors Elizabeth Carwright and Delvac Oceandy. The ideal candidate will have excellent experimental, organisational and communication skills, and will be capable of working both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team. Experience of cardiomyocyte biology, in-vivo small animal model work and /or mammalian cell culture, molecular and cell biology, and mechanistic investigation of signalling pathways using genetic and/or pharmacological approaches is essential. Experience in the analysis of mitochondrial function, advanced imaging and therapeutic intervention studies would be advantageous. Successful candidates may be subject to pre-employment screening carried out on our behalf by a third party. The offer of employment will be dependent on the successful candidate passing that screening. Whilst you will be required to provide express consent at a later stage, by continuing with your application now you acknowledge that you are aware that such screening will take place, and agree to take part in the process. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Ashraf Kitmitto Email Address: ashraf.kitmitto@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46063": {
      "title": "Research Associate on the Mechanics of Floating Structures",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate on the Mechanics of Floating Structures Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate on the Mechanics of Floating Structures Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032408 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Physics & Astronomy Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 27/10/2026 Contract Duration: 7 months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Post-doctoral Research Associate. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to research on the mechanics of floating structures. The role will focus on building mathematical models of fluid-structure interactions, developing new experimental activities and analysing data, and will take place within a dynamic and inclusive research environment. You will be responsible for * Performing experiments on floating structures. * Acquiring & analysing experimental data and interpreting findings. * Using mathematical techniques to model fluid-structure interactions. * Writing up research work for publication. * Working with colleagues on joint projects We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: * Hold or be about to obtain a PhD (or equivalent) in physics, engineering, chemistry or applied mathematics. * Strong problem-solving skills, and eagerness to learn new skills. * Ability to work collaboratively within a group and, also, to work independently. * Excellent written and oral communication skills. Desirable Criteria: * Demonstrable experience researching fluid-structure interactions. * Demonstrable experience in mechanical modelling. * Demonstrable experience performing experiments on soft and/or slender structures. * Analytical understanding of elastic buckling (in)stabilities. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Finn Box Email Address: finn.box@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46156": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Digital Design",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Postdoctoral Research Associate in Digital Design Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Postdoctoral Research Associate in Digital Design Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032496 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 12/10/2026 Contract Duration: 30 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Job Description The University of Manchester is inviting applications for a Post-Doctoral Research Associate to lead the design of next-generation vision processor hardware. This role focuses on near-sensor integration of novel computer architectures for advanced edge computing scenarios. The research involves design space exploration and the implementation of digital systems using FPGAs, with potential for future ASIC implementation. You will carry out system modelling, design novel parallel processor architectures at the RTL level (using Verilog/SystemVerilog or VHDL), and drive the hardware-software co-design aspects of these new architectures. Additionally, the role involves interfacing with high-speed sensors and building practical technology demonstrations. This position is embedded within a long-standing research programme on vision sensors and SIMD processor arrays in the laboratory of Prof Piotr Dudek at the University of Manchester. The role is directly associated with the EPSRC-funded project on \"On-sensor Computer Vision,\" carried out in collaboration with Imperial College London, University of Bristol, and industrial partners. As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Piotr Dudek Email: p.dudek@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46061": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Rock Mechanics & Engineering",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Rock Mechanics &amp; Engineering Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Rock Mechanics & Engineering Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032407 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Civil Engineering and Management Hours Per Week: Full time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 12/10/2026 Contract Duration: 18 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a self-motivated and collaborative individual to join our Rock Mechanics and Geomechanics Engineering team as a Postdoctoral Research Associate. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to an externally funded research project addressing key challenges in subsurface engineering and sustainable energy development. You will be based in the Department of Civil Engineering and Management at The University of Manchester and work within a wider interdisciplinary research team with strong academic and industrial collaboration. The position is initially funded for 18 months, with the possibility of extension subject to further funding. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Junlong Shang Email: junlong.shang@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46142": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Sustainability Assessment in Retrofitssociate in Sustainability Assessment in Retrofit",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Sustainability Assessment in Retrofitssociate in Sustainability Assessment in Retrofit Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Sustainability Assessment in Retrofitssociate in Sustainability Assessment in Retrofit Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032477 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Materials Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 26/10/2026 Contract Duration: 42 months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description This Research Associate position forms part of the Retrofit Advanced Materials & Manufacturing Innovation Centre (RAMMIC), a major collaborative research and innovation programme supported through the UKRI Local Innovation Partnership Fund. Led by the University of Salford’s Energy House Labs, RAMMIC brings together Greater Manchester capabilities in building performance, advanced materials, indoor air quality, acoustics, environmental sustainability assessment, and innovation support. Its purpose is to establish a collaborative innovation ecosystem for smart and sustainable retrofit products and processes. RAMMIC will work with businesses to overcome technical and commercial barriers to retrofit innovation. Research and innovation activities will make use of the Energy House Labs facilities, including existing and newly developed test houses in controlled environment warehouses. The programme will evaluate new products and services under controlled conditions, generate reliable evidence of their performance and environmental effects, quantify environmental impacts across marketable comparators, and support promising innovations towards commercialisation and wider deployment. The programme will address challenges associated with the building envelope, heating and cooling systems, ventilation, material selection, commissioning, indoor air quality and the assessment of retrofit performance. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status.  All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Michael Shaver Email: michael.shaver@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45954": {
      "title": "Senior Technician (Textiles-Knitting)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Technician (Textiles-Knitting) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Technician (Textiles-Knitting) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032295 Salary: £33,002 - £36,636 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 12/10/2026 Contract Duration: 13 months (contract length dependant on start date) School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Overall Purpose A Knitting Technician is required within the University’s teaching area and laboratory, ensuring continuity of technical and teaching support for students and academic staff during the period of absence. The role is essential to the safe and effective operation of knitting machinery, equipment and associated facilities, as well as supporting the delivery of practical classes and maintaining a high-quality learning environment. The overall purpose of the role is to provide technical support and contribute to the delivery of classes, including preparing and maintaining equipment, demonstrating knitting techniques and machinery, guiding and supporting students during practical sessions, and providing technical advice for student projects. The postholder will also ensure that equipment and facilities are appropriately maintained and that relevant health and safety procedures are followed within the teaching laboratory. The Knitting Technician will provide technical and teaching support within the University’s teaching area and laboratory, delivering practical classes and demonstrations in knitting. They will support students and academic staff with the use of knitting machinery, equipment and materials, assist with student projects, and ensure the teaching environment is safe, well-organised and fit for purpose. The role holder will use their knowledge and practical experience of knitting technologies to deliver engaging and effective practical classes, demonstrations and one-to-one support. They will prepare equipment and materials in advance of teaching, maintain and troubleshoot knitting machinery, provide guidance on techniques and processes, and work collaboratively with academic colleagues to support the delivery of the curriculum. They will also ensure appropriate health and safety procedures are followed and that equipment is maintained to a suitable standard. The role will ensure continuity of high-quality technical teaching and student support during maternity leave, enabling students to develop practical, technical and creative skills with confidence. The postholder will make a direct contribution to the student learning experience by providing expertise, facilitating access to industry-relevant equipment and techniques, and helping students successfully realise their creative and academic projects. (Grades 1-5) Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Mark Chadwick Teaching Technical Specialist Email Address: mark.chadwick@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below . Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45911": {
      "title": "Head of School Finance (Snr Business Partner)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Head of School Finance (Snr Business Partner) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Head of School Finance (Snr Business Partner) Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032259 Salary: £47,389 - £58,225 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Manchester Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Financial Analysis and Reporting Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 16/10/2026 Contract Duration: N/A School/Directorate: Directorate of Finance Job Description Overall Purpose of the Job The Head of School/Directorate Finance is a member of the Directorate of Finance working under the guidance of the Deputy Director of Faculty Finance. You will work alongside Heads of School/Directors/Senior Managers as a business partner to provide operational and strategic support on all financial matters. You will be responsible for managing the financial planning, budgeting, forecasting and reporting processes for your area of responsibility. This will include analysis of performance and highlighting remedial action where required. You will develop and continuously improve management information to assist School/Directorate budget holders. You will be customer focused, flexible in your approach, and work in conjunction with other finance colleagues, covering for and relating with them in order to develop and enhance your role and the service delivered by the Directorate of Finance. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Vicky Skinner Email Address: victoria.skinner@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032259 HEAD OF SCHOOL FINANCE (SNR BUSINESS PARTNER) FP'S (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45981": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Polymeric Materials Modelling",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Postdoctoral Research Associate in Polymeric Materials Modelling Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Postdoctoral Research Associate in Polymeric Materials Modelling Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032320 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Materials Hours Per Week: Full Time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 26/10/2026 Contract Duration: 24 Months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description A senior postdoctoral position is available for an ambitious individual to carry out the molecular modelling of cellulose oligomers investigating the self-assembly of the polymer in the plant cell wall. The senior postdoctoral assistant will also assist another postdoctoral researcher in the day-to-day supervisorial activities for Eichhorn’s wider group of diverse PhD and MSc students, including project supervision, health and safety, and assistance with the drafting of manuscripts. The successful applicant will undertake a fundamental study of the assembly of cellulose, using a unique approach of cellulose oligomers that have surfactant-like properties. There have many recent attempts to synthesize cellulose analogues in the laboratory, through self-assembly methods using enzymatic produced oligomeric forms. This work will pave the way for the practical assembly of cellulose oligomeric forms by screening large arrays of structures with different moieties and charges. To effectively do this, the postdoctoral researcher will combine machine learning, molecular dynamics simulations and high performance computing (Isambard AI). Applicants must have a PhD in an appropriate area of computational chemistry or a related discipline and experience in the molecular modelling of polymers, polysaccharides, and preferably cellulose. Informal enquiries are welcome and should be made to Steve Eichhorn (Email: s.j.eichhorn@bristol.ac.uk). You will be responsible for: The conducting of high-quality and publishable research in the area of the molecular modelling of cellulose and related materials e.g. other plant cell wall polymers. This will involve the implementation, execution and analysis of advanced molecular simulations, the use of machine learning techniques and deployment on state-of-the-art high performance computing platforms. Through interaction with the PI and other members of the team, contributing proactively to the development, progression and execution of the project Analysing and interpreting data and make a significant input to the scientific direction of the project Preparation and presentation of regular reports on research progress We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Ph.D. in computational chemistry, computational materials science or a relevant discipline Proven research skills Scientific scripting / programming Experience in molecular dynamics simulations of polymers Desirable Criteria: Molecular modelling and simulation experience during PhD Ability to present complex information effectively to a range of audiences Experience of manipulating and visualizing large datasets We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Professor Steve Eichhorn Email Address: s.j.eichhorn@bristol.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date Further particulars (with person specification) linked below Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46091": {
      "title": "Student Finance Administrator – University Funded Schemes",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Student Finance Administrator – University Funded Schemes Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Student Finance Administrator – University Funded Schemes Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032435 Salary: £29,588 to £32,080 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Student and Academic Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 22/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate for the Student Experience Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Overall Purpose of the Job: Be responsible for the administration of activities for the assigned area; supervise and co-ordinate the work of the team, acting as a point of reference. Provide information, advice and guidance to stakeholders. Work flexibly as required across the University, supporting delivery of University and local priorities as a single cross-University team. Maintain own data management, the student record and data quality for all related areas of delivery. Maintain and update system records with reference to current data protection legislation and provide reports. Assist with the planning, organisation and delivery of events; organise and service meetings. Act in accordance with and promote University policies and requirements at all times. Deliver excellent customer service and play an active role in communities of practice. Assist with the delivery and monitoring of projects and activities. Be part of the collaborative student finance team taking ownership of student finance enquiries within specified service level agreements. Process Hardship Fund applications and other University funding applications in a timely manner and in line with guidance and allocated budget. Undertake administrative and financial processes for schemes requiring evaluation by a panel of staff; undertake administration of loans in respect of US students, having detailed knowledge of regulations governing the US Loan Program and private US loans. Have knowledge of the administration of Veteran's Affairs (VA) benefits in respect of US military veterans and/or their children. Generate requests for refund, investigate and resolve as appropriate; communicate with current and prospective students regarding their loans; prepare and submit online bi-monthly enrolment reports to National Student Loan Database System (NSLDS). Please note, under the new immigration rules this role is not eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker route of the Points Based System. Candidates will need to be able to demonstrate their right to work in the UK in order to be eligible to take up the post. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to People.Recruitment@manchester.ac.uk . Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Taibah Khan Email: taibah.khan@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: People.recruitment@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: Jobtrain: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032435 - Student Finance Administrator FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45850": {
      "title": "Regulatory Compliance Officer (Maternity cover)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Regulatory Compliance Officer (Maternity cover) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Regulatory Compliance Officer (Maternity cover) Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032333 Salary: £37,694-£46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Compliance and Risk Office Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 15/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 13 month School/Directorate: Directorate of Compliance and Risk Job Description The University of Manchester is the largest single site university in the UK and one of the top universities in the world with over 10,000 staff and 50,000 students. We are seeking to recruit a full-time Regulatory Compliance Officer who will work closely with and support the Head of Research Security and Compliance in delivering a service to facilitate cutting-edge research activity, within the envelope of national and international export controls and other regulatory areas. This is a complex area of international regulation and requires the ability to assimilate, understand and translate those regulations to a range of audiences. More importantly perhaps, is the ability to engage, support and work effectively with colleagues from a wide range of backgrounds, working in such areas as advanced materials, aerospace biology and nuclear. The postholder will be expected to compile and submit applications through the government portals and liaise with the different government departments on an as required basis. The successful applicant will have knowledge of academic research environment. They will also have an analytical mind-set and attention to detail and awareness of legislation and regulations associated with research activities. In addition, they will have an awareness of the regulations associated with trade compliance and governance issues; and have experience of briefing senior managers and reporting to senior staff/committees. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Irene Barinaga-Rementeria Ramirez Email Address: Irene.barinaga@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032333 Regulatory Compliance Officer (Maternity cover) FP's (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45778": {
      "title": "Assistant Technician (Cleanrooms)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Assistant Technician (Cleanrooms) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Assistant Technician (Cleanrooms) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032175 Salary: UoM Grade 3 £27,319 - £28,778 Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 15/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Assistant Technician (Cleanrooms) plays a key role in supporting the day-to-day operations of cleanroom facilities across FSE. Working to established procedures, the role holder will carry out routine operational checks, deliver cleanroom inductions, manage consumable inventory, assist researchers and promote safe working practices. They will work collaboratively with cleanroom colleagues and other professional services teams including Estates, Infrastructure & Facilities, Operations and Safety Services. Through this work, they will help maintain a safe, organised and responsible cleanroom ecosystem that supports excellent technical service delivery and enables high-quality research across the Faculty. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to People.Recruitment@manchester.ac.uk . Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Please note this role is not eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker route of the Points Based System. Candidates will need to be able to demonstrate their right to work in the UK in order to be eligible to take up the post. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Dr Kunal Lulla (Senior Technical Specialist – Cleanrooms) Email: kunal.lulla@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: People.recruitment@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: Jobtrain: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45982": {
      "title": "KTP Associate in Development of Low-carbon Bricks",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for KTP Associate in Development of Low-carbon Bricks Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results KTP Associate in Development of Low-carbon Bricks Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032321 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Civil Engineering and Management Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 21/10/2026 Contract Duration: 34 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description This is an exciting opportunity to play a leading role in delivering a high-impact 34-month Knowledge Transfer Partnership (KTP) project with the University of Manchester (UoM) and Michelmersh Brick UK Ltd., focused on decarbonising clay brick manufacturing. The project aims to develop and validate a sustainable bio-based alternative to fossil derived body fuel for use in premium clamp fired products, supporting the transition to lower carbon manufacturing. The KTP Associate will coordinate activity across manufacturing operations, technical teams, academic researchers and external partners, translating research into practical solutions that can be implemented at scale within a live production environment. The role will involve planning and leading trials, ensuring product quality, process stability and supply resilience are maintained throughout. Part funded through Innovate UK’s KTP programme, this role offers a unique opportunity to deliver meaningful industrial and environmental impact while developing technical leadership, project management and innovation delivery experience. The Associate will be based primarily at Michelmersh’s Freshfield Lane site near Haywards Heath, Sussex, with periods spent at UoM to support collaboration and access specialist research facilities. (The Associate’s time in the lab in Manchester is likely to be split across 3 trips of 2-3 months duration each.) Provisional interview dates: 3rd and 6th of November Due to the nature of the funding, KTP Associates who have already completed a KTP are not eligible to apply. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Meini Su Email: meini.su@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46012": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Mathematical Biomedicine",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Mathematical Biomedicine Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Mathematical Biomedicine Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032414 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Integrative Physiology Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 23/10/2026 Contract Duration: 33 months School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are excited to offer this opportunity to a highly motivated Research Associate to join an ambitious new team working in the field of Digital Endocrinology. The successful applicant will conduct original research aimed at translating understanding of endocrine function into precision medicine applications. The research will use data from wearable device technologies to characterise variability in circadian hormone profiles in humans, uncovering fundamental insights about endocrine rhythms that can be used to support the diagnosis and management of endocrine conditions. The position is funded through a UKRI Future Leaders Fellowship awarded to Dr Eder Zavala: “Developing a mathematical and computational framework for digital endocrinology”. The work will build on hormonal time series data collected at unprecedented time resolution in healthy humans and in patients, including studies in real life settings. We are seeking to appoint a creative and enthusiastic individual who will lead on computational data analysis projects that interrogate the mechanisms behind hormonal rhythm misalignment. The research will be closely integrated with mathematical models that predict dynamic responses to perturbations (e.g., stressors, sleep disruptions, diagnostic tests), as well as the long-term changes that occur during disease. A key challenge of your work will be accounting for the variability in hormonal rhythms observed across individuals. To characterise this variability, you will take advantage of unique in its kind datasets and advanced mathematical and computational methods and help develop personalised models. The practical element of your project will be based on, but not limited to, time series analysis, network analysis, Bayesian inference, Machine Learning, as well as computational simulation of mathematical models written in terms of differential equations. In addition to leading their own research projects, the appointed candidate will have the opportunity to contribute to the projects of PhD students in the group, as well as with an international team of collaborators including clinicians, computer scientists, and mathematicians. The team is dynamic and ambitious as well as being welcoming and supportive. The applicant should have high proficiency with scientific computer programming in Python, Julia, R and/or MATLAB, parameter estimation methods and HPC simulations. Experience with dynamical systems theory and mathematical modelling using ODEs and/or DDEs is desirable. The vacancy is for a full-time post available from 1st February 2027 until 31st December 2029. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Dr Eder Zavala (UKRI Future Leaders Fellow; Senior Lecturer in Mathematical Biomedicine) Email: eder.zavala@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45459": {
      "title": "Integrated Designer",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Integrated Designer Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Integrated Designer Apply for job Job reference: CIX-032351 Salary: UoM Grade 5 £33,002 - £36,636 pro rata depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Cultural Institutions Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Whitworth Art Gallery Administration Hours Per Week: 21 hours per week (0.6 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 22/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Whitworth Art Gallery Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description *CV, Cover letter & portfolio of own work required. When submitting your application, please upload as one document under 1MB. .We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Designer at The Whitworth . This role offers an exciting opportunity to create high-quality visual design for the Whitworth’s exhibitions, programmes and communications across print and digital platforms. The role will support the delivery of marketing campaigns, digital content and audience engagement activity by producing compelling visual assets that reflect the Whitworth’s identity and align with both our and The University of Manchester’s brand guidelines. You will be responsible for: Produce creative design solutions for exhibition campaigns, digital content, social media, print collateral and promotional materials. Prepare artwork for print and digital production, ensuring files meet technical specifications and brand standards. Design visual assets that support storytelling and audience engagement across multiple platforms. Support the delivery of exhibition and programme campaigns by producing design assets for web, email, social media and print. Work with colleagues across curatorial, programming and engagement teams to interpret briefs and deliver effective design solutions. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Demonstrate strong design skills with experience producing visual content across both print and digital formats. Have experience using Adobe Creative Suite (particularly InDesign, Illustrator and Photoshop). Demonstrate strong typography, layout and visual communication skills. Be able to interpret design briefs and translate them into effective visual solutions. Show a commitment to equality, diversity and inclusion and to representing diverse audiences through visual communications. Desirable Criteria: Experience working in a cultural, arts, museum or higher education environment. Experience producing design for digital and social media platforms. Experience creating simple motion graphics or video assets. Familiarity with asset management systems or digital publishing platforms. Experience working with external suppliers such as printers or photographers. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Rakhi Sinha Jones Email Address: rakhi.sinhajones@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt CIX-032351 JD (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45960": {
      "title": "Technician (Metallurgy)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Technician (Metallurgy) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Technician (Metallurgy) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032301 Salary: £29,588 to £32,080 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 13/10/2026 Contract Duration: 12 months (dependent of start date- contract must end on 17/09/2027) School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Interviews: Monday 2nd November This role will support a variety of different areas within the Faculty of Science and Engineering with specific focus on the heat treatment of a range of materials and the thermomechanical processing of metals and alloys. The facilities in this area are used to support world-class research, teaching and work with industry. Part of your role will be to run and maintain a suite of furnaces, working alone or with the support or advice of other technicians, academic staff, or students. You will train users on the operation of the equipment and help with appropriate heat treatments to support their project and material needs. This role is varied and hands on, with work ranging from preparing specimens, supporting large-scale thermomechanical testing to complex heat treatment schedules, maintaining equipment, training facility users and contributing to innovative research projects. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to People.Recruitment@manchester.ac.uk . Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Please note this role is not eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker route of the Points Based System. Candidates will need to be able to demonstrate their right to work in the UK in order to be eligible to take up the post. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Ross Nolan Email: ross.nolan@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: People.recruitment@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: Jobtrain: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45843": {
      "title": "Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Assistant (Programmes & Curriculum)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Assistant (Programmes &amp; Curriculum) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Assistant (Programmes & Curriculum) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032190 Salary: £27,319 - £28,778 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: School of Biological Sciences Admin Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 12/10/2026 Contract Duration: N/A - Permanent School/Directorate: School of Biological Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Overall Purpose Providing clerical and administrative support for teaching, learning and student administration within the Programmes and Curriculum team. Working accurately, flexibly and collaboratively, the role supports effective services, reliable student records and clear communication. The post contributes to an excellent student experience and to continuous improvement across Professional Services. About the Team The Programmes and Curriculum team sits within the School of Biological Sciences in the Faculty of Biology, Medicine and Health. The team supports teaching, learning and student administration for students and academic colleagues. The role reports to a Teaching and Learning Coordinator and works collaboratively across School and University boundaries to deliver local and University priorities as part of a single Professional Services team. Key Accountabilities • Providing administrative support for teaching, learning and student administration, as directed by the Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator. • Responding as a first point of contact for routine enquiries, providing clear information and guidance, and escalating matters when appropriate. • Maintaining accurate student records, managing data responsibly and supporting high standards of data quality. • Preparing correspondence and maintaining accurate records and documentation for local processes. • Supporting financial administration, including placing orders for goods and services in line with University procedures. • Organising meetings, room bookings and catering, preparing and circulating papers, and taking minutes where required. • Supporting the delivery of projects, team activities, policies and procedures, with a focus on continuous improvement. • Producing appropriate management information, data and analysis to support the work of the team. • Delivering excellent and inclusive customer service to students, colleagues and other stakeholders. • Working flexibly and collaboratively across teams, contributing to communities of practice and helping to create a joined-up student experience. • Following and promoting University requirements relating to health and safety, equality, diversity and inclusion, information governance and financial procedures. Our benefits include: (Grades 1-5) Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Charlotte Jenkins Email Address: charlotte.jenkins@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46036": {
      "title": "Senior Technician (Civil and Mechanical Engineering)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Technician (Civil and Mechanical Engineering) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Technician (Civil and Mechanical Engineering) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032381 Salary: £33,002 - £36,636 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 27/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Senior Technician (Civil and Mechanical Engineering). This role offers an exciting opportunity to provide expert technical support across our Fluids, Heavy Structures, Dynamics, Impact/Ballistics, Geotechnical and Concrete Processing laboratories. Using your technical knowledge and problem-solving skills, you will support safe, efficient and high-quality teaching, research and testing activities within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: To work closely with undergraduate/postgraduate students to support them in conducting experimental work in the areas of heavy structures, dynamics, fluids and testing equipment. This will include supporting them in setting up test rigs and measurement chains, conducting experiments and validating test results. Demonstrating and supporting delivery of practical classes as part of taught programs. Including but not limited to Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering and Civil Engineering and Management 4th Year and PGT Mechanical Engineering Group Design Projects, IMechE grand challenges and Design, Manufacture and Test modules. To train or instruct other staff and students in the use of equipment and it’s servicing as required and within the limits of technical competence. Working collaboratively with colleagues and stakeholders, help resolve technical challenges, maintain and develop facilities and equipment, and identify opportunities to improve our services. Supervise, motivate and develop junior staff and University of Manchester technical apprentices. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria A qualification in a science or engineering-related subject area e.g. degree, HND, HNC, OR significant work experience in a relevant technical, engineering or scientific role Experience in fabrication and testing methods used in the application of Civil and Mechanical Engineering. Demonstrable experience in engineering elements like functionality, replicability, and costs in relation to the design and how they are applied in the completion of engineering projects Extensive knowledge in the use of workshop machine tools necessary, for fabrication and sample preparation, including: lathes, CNC milling, grinding, cutting, grinding, polishing and drilling machines An aptitude for training and mentoring students from a diverse range of backgrounds Desirable Criteria Demonstrable experience in the operation of furnace control systems, data loggers, and the application of fire engineering practice Experience of strain gauging and mechanical property testing of concrete, composite and metallic systems Experience of working with specialist IT packages/software such as LabCup, PPMS and CAD/CAM for example, SolidWorks Knowledge of teaching and research operations gained from within a Higher Education setting Line management / supervisory experience We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Jacob Skelly Email Address: jacob.skelly@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK Applications close at midnight on the closing date Further particulars (with person specification) linked below Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45844": {
      "title": "Assistant Technician (SUGT, PGT, Pharmacy Labs)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Assistant Technician (SUGT, PGT, Pharmacy Labs) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Assistant Technician (SUGT, PGT, Pharmacy Labs) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032192 Salary: £27,319 - £28,778 (pro rata) per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FBMH Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 (39 weeks per year - term time, 0.75FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 14/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Assistant Technician (SUGT, PGR Teaching) – 39 weeks per year, pro-rata The Stopford Undergraduate Teaching Laboratories (SUGT) based in the Stopford Building is a large suite of labs which supports practical teaching for all undergraduate students within the Faculty of Biology, Medicine & Health. This is an exciting opportunity for a hardworking individual with a good scientific background to contribute to a service which values student experience above all else. The main purpose of this role is to provide technical support as required, as well as to ensure that the equipment and consumables required for delivery of our practical sessions is well stocked & maintained and available, depending on the skills being taught. Housekeeping will also be an important aspect of the role. You would be working as part of a technical team to support specific student practical classes. Because we operate a flexible and agile technical service you could be required to work across undergraduate, postgraduate and pharmacy laboratory teams, depending on the needs of the service. Please note, this is a term-time only role and the pay will be 75% of the full-time equivalent (pro-rata). (Grades 1-5) Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Successful candidates may be subject to pre-employment screening carried out on our behalf by a third party. The offer of employment will be dependent on the successful candidate passing that screening. Whilst you will be required to provide express consent at a later stage, by continuing with your application now you acknowledge that you are aware that such screening will take place, and agree to take part in the process. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Nicola Steel Email: nicola.steel@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45880": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032225 Salary: £37,694 - £41,064 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Physics & Astronomy Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 14/10/2026 Contract Duration: 36 months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Applications are invited for the position of 36-month PDRA in the Biological Physics Group, Department of Physics and Astronomy, the University of Manchester. We are seeking to appoint an individual with research skills in biointerface and biocolloid to undertake a 36-month research project to study the interactions of biocides with microbial membranes using physical and biochemical approaches including neutron reflection and scattering. The successful applicant is expected to have existing experience in surface and colloidal science, antimicrobial research and the techniques, and a proven track record in publication. The role will report to Prof Jian R Lu. The successful applicant would be a highly motivated individual with proven record in conducting membrane based antimicrobial research and a PhD in biophysics, surface science or equivalent. Along with your application, you should submit a single file containing your CV, list of publications, and research statement (max 2 pages). The Department of Physics and Astronomy is committed to promoting Equality, Diversity, Inclusion and Access through contributing to the University’s social responsibility agenda, demonstrating a commitment to its policies, activities and delivery of initiatives including the Athena SWAN charter for promoting women’s careers in STEMM subjects (science, technology, engineering, mathematics and medicine) in higher education. The Department has held JUNO Champion status since 2016 for its commitment to achieving gender equality which positively promotes inclusivity for all. As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from diverse career paths and backgrounds and from all sections of the community regardless of age, disability, ethnicity, gender, gender expression, sexual orientation and transgender status. Appointment will be made on merit and equal opportunities data completed by applicants will not be accessible by the Recruiting Manager and panel. We support applications from those returning from a career break and are happy to discuss flexible working arrangements. For further information, please visit http://www.physics.manchester.ac.uk/about-us/equality-and-diversity/ (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Jian R Lu Email: j.lu@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45977": {
      "title": "Venture Building Performance Manager",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Venture Building Performance Manager Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Venture Building Performance Manager Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032317 Salary: £59,966 to £71,566 per annum, depending on relevant experience+ market supplement Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Unit M Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 14/10/2026 Contract Duration: 18 Months School/Directorate: Unit M Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Venture Builder performance manager will operate within Unit M, directly supporting Innovate UK’s start-up ecosystem team and specifically the ICURe programme. The role will take responsibility as the Performance Manager for the IUK Venture Builder pilot programme. Some of the things you might find yourself doing include: Lead delivery and performance management of the £1.5m Innovate UK Venture Builder pilot, targeting IS6 technologies, acting as the primary strategic contact for delivery partners and Monitoring Officers. Own programme impact analysis and reporting, providing expert insights and quarterly performance updates to Innovate UK’s Executive Team. Drive Phase 2 procurement and partner selection, including defining scope across IS6 technologies, leading tender activities, and designing and assessing bid evaluation criteria. Monitor programme performance and governance, tracking metrics, cohort progress, milestones, and reporting to governance boards and senior stakeholders. Accelerate operational effectiveness, identifying bottlenecks and implementing data-driven improvements to business and technical processes. Build strategic partnerships and alignment, working across Innovate UK, UKRI, Unit M, sector teams, and the wider innovation ecosystem to ensure programme success and knowledge sharing. What You’ll Bring: Proven experience leading and performance-managing national delivery partners and complex stakeholder relationships across public and private sector organisations. Track record of developing and delivering large-scale, complex programmes and projects to agreed time, cost, and quality standards. Strong knowledge of the UK research commercialisation and spin-out ecosystem, including universities, research institutes, PSREs, and regional/national pathways. Deep understanding of early-stage venture creation and investment readiness, including spin-out challenges, venture de-risking, pre-seed/seed funding, and investor due diligence. Strategic leadership with operational delivery capability, able to build partnerships, align stakeholders, solve delivery challenges, and drive impact. Strong analytical and communication skills, including portfolio/performance management, dashboard reporting, stakeholder engagement, and presenting insights to senior audiences. What You’ll Get: 29 days annual leave, plus bank holidays and December closure days Generous pension scheme Flexible and hybrid working Access to a wide range of staff benefits, including discounts, wellbeing support, and development programmes Explore full benefits: www.manchester.ac.uk/connect/jobs/benefits-working-here As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Vicky Roth Email: vicky.roth@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. For informal enquiries please contact the Talent Attraction Team people.talent@manchester.ac.uk Supporting Documents save_alt Venture Building Performance Manager Job Description (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45984": {
      "title": "PGR Operations Officer (Recruitment &amp; Admissions)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for PGR Operations Officer (Recruitment &amp; Admissions) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results PGR Operations Officer (Recruitment & Admissions) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032324 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FBMH Research and Business Engagement Support Services Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 16/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Interviews will be held on Monday 2 November. This role leads the delivery of postgraduate research (PGR) recruitment and admissions activity within the Doctoral Academy. The postholder contributes to the development of recruitment strategy and works closely with the PGR Services Manager and with the Marketing, Planning and Finance teams to support delivery against student number targets. The postholder manages the Doctoral Academy’s Recruitment and Admissions Team, providing operational oversight of policy, processes, reporting, and compliance. They are responsible for ensuring the effective management of all admissions and selection processes. Working collaboratively with senior stakeholders, the postholder oversees the allocation of studentship funding, liaising with recruiting supervisors, industry partners, and colleagues in Finances. The postholder also plays a key role in enhancing the applicant experience, ensuring a high-quality, consistent approach to recruitment and admissions delivery. More broadly, they contribute to a culture of excellence and continuous improvement across Professional Services, supporting a flexible and collaborative approach that places the postgraduate researcher experience at the centre of all activity. Colleagues will be expected to work flexibly across the University, taking on assignments and roles as required, underpinned by the principles of business partnering and collaborative working. This approach supports the delivery of both University-wide and local priorities, with colleagues operating as part of a single, cross-University team. Assignments may include work across: PGR progression and support; PGR programmes; PGR funding and programmes. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Tasleem Hanif Email: tasleem.hanif@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-032324 - PGR Operations Officer (Recruitment & Admissions) FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45946": {
      "title": "TEACHING LEARNING STUDENT EXPERIENCE (TLSE) ASSISTANT (INFORMATION, ADVICE AND GUIDANCE/STUDENT SUPPORT AND WELLBEING)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for TEACHING LEARNING STUDENT EXPERIENCE (TLSE) ASSISTANT    (INFORMATION, ADVICE AND GUIDANCE/STUDENT SUPPORT AND WELLBEING) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results TEACHING LEARNING STUDENT EXPERIENCE (TLSE) ASSISTANT    (INFORMATION, ADVICE AND GUIDANCE/STUDENT SUPPORT AND WELLBEING) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032287 Salary: £27,319 - £28,778 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Jean McFarlane Building, University of Manchester Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: School of Health Sciences Administration Hours Per Week: 35 (full-time) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 13/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Applications are invited for the post of Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Assistant, within the Student Hub in the School of Health Sciences. This role forms part of the School Teaching, Learning and Student Experience team and provides support to students across our Undergraduate and Postgraduate programmes. This will also include liaising with academic staff, other Professional Services function teams and students. We are seeking to appoint an individual who is motivated to work within a customer service environment and ideally has some understanding of the academic and pastoral support procedures and processes included with supporting undergraduate and postgraduate study. Acting as a first point of contact for student enquiries and student support and wellbeing related matters, this is a student facing role which requires on-campus presence for the majority of the working week (usually 3 working days, with 2 days from home). Based within our Student Hub, the successful candidate will be supporting students with a range of enquiries both in person, over the phone and via our email inboxes and supporting the administration of a range of activities including (but not limited to): supporting Mitigating Circumstances and Extensions processes, student experience initiatives and events and working on the student hub. The ideal candidate will be someone who has a strong administrative background in a busy environment, customer service experience and holds excellent communication skills and the ability to work positively and respectfully with a range of students and colleagues from diverse backgrounds. You will need to have a flexible approach to work and the ability to work proficiently with a range of MS 365 applications. Professional Services staff are part of a single, University-wide team and are expected to work flexibly across organisational boundaries to support local and institutional priorities. (Grades 1-5) Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Guy Malkin Email: Guy.Malkin@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46002": {
      "title": "Senior Administrator",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Administrator Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Administrator Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032345 Salary: UoM Grade 5 £33,002 - £36,636 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Jean McFarlane Building, University of Manchester Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: School of Health Sciences Administration Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 15/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are inviting applications for the post of Senior Administrative in the School of Health Sciences. The post will be based in the Division of Population Health, Health Services Research and Primary Care and the Division of Informatics, Imaging and Data Sciences on the main University campus. The primary aim of this role is to provide comprehensive and proactive administrative support for activities within the Divisions. You will be a key administrative point of contact for the Divisions and will ensure questions, issues and concerns are dealt with effectively. The post holder will manage administrative support across the Divisions ensuring that the administrative support of Division Leads, Divisional Operations Manager, academic staff and students are met with efficiency. The role holder will be working in collaboration with colleagues across the University and externally. You will have good organisational skills and be able to prioritise work in a busy environment to meet deadlines; have excellent communication skills and be flexible and responsive to customer needs; be able to identify problems and resolve issues effectively; ability to work on own initiative and have good computer skills, including proficiency in Microsoft Office software. Our benefits include: (Grades 1-5) Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Raj Rana Email: rajeshree.rana@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-032345  Senior Administrator FP'S (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45926": {
      "title": "Accreditation &amp; Rankings Manager",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Accreditation &amp; Rankings Manager Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Accreditation & Rankings Manager Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032280 Salary: £47,389 - £58,225 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Manchester Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Alliance MBS Administration Hours Per Week: Full Time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 20/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Alliance Manchester Business School Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as an Accreditation & Rankings Manager. The Accreditation & Rankings Manager has a key strategic and operational role within Alliance Manchester Business School. They support the Head of School, Director of School Operations and Partnership Director in the development and implementation of the School’s accreditation strategy and reaccreditation cycle as well as the School’s strategic approach to external rankings along with their operational delivery. You will be responsible for: Reporting to the Partnership Director and supporting the Head of School and the Director of School Operations in the development, implementation, execution and monitoring of the School’s strategy, policies, procedures and priorities relating to external accreditation standards, ensuring a key aim of continuous improvement is embedded across the School To take overall responsibility for the consistent delivery of the reaccreditation cycle, including writing five yearly submissions, external mid-term progress reports and annual progress reports for each Standard across the School’s activities To manage and coordinate all peer review visits, including all preparations, correspondence, continuous data requirements and delivery of post accreditation actions Lead on the annual Business School Rankings cycle, act as secretary to the Strategic Rankings Committee and work with the Partnership Director and other key stakeholders providing analysis and insight to support strategic decision making in relation to rankings Lead on operational delivery of the rankings strategy, leading cross functional groups to coordinate our rankings submissions and communications We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: A degree or equivalent professional experience in a relevant field Knowledge of business school accreditations and rankings Development of large, complex reports, presenting at senior levels, managing a range of stakeholders and leading and motivating teams without line authority Strong relationship management, stakeholder engagement, communication, facilitation and influencing skills Exceptional writing skills, critical thinking and the ability to understand data Desirable Criteria: Wider knowledge of the business school landscape both in the UK and internationally Project management, setting and achieving agreed deadlines Evidence of delivering continuous improvement Digitally literate, comfortable with a range of IT tools We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Chris Garnett Email Address: chris.garnett@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt HUM-032280 - Accreditation & Rankings Manager - FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45861": {
      "title": "Collections Engagement Officer",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Collections Engagement Officer Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Collections Engagement Officer Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032209 Salary: UoM Grade 5 £33,002 - £36,636 pro rata depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Ahmed Iqbal Ullah RACE Centre The University of Manchester, Manchester Central Library, 3rd Floor, St Peter’s Square, Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Special Collections Hours Per Week: 28 hours per week (0.8 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 01/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term from 1 February 2027 (or earlier if available), until Monday 8 November 2027 School/Directorate: The University of Manchester Library Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join the team as a Collections Engagement Officer on a fixed-term contract providing maternity cover. Based within the Ahmed Iqbal Ullah RACE Centre , you will work to increase knowledge, value and use of the Centre’s rich library and archive collections through inclusive and creative engagement activities. While the role has a natural focus on academic audiences, you will work with schools and young people, community organisations and other cultural and heritage sector organisations, and the general public. The role requires someone who can embed racial and social justice principles in creative collections-based engagement work. Contract details This is a 0.8 FTE role (28 hours per week). The contract will start on 1 February 2027 (though an earlier start is possible, dependent on the selected candidate's availability) and will end on Monday 8 November 2027. Interviews will take place on Monday 23 November and Friday 27 November 2026 (afternoons). Interview questions will be provided in advance. You will be responsible for: - Increasing knowledge, value and use of the Ahmed Iqbal Ullah RACE Centre's library and archive collections through inclusive and creative engagement activities. - Working with academic audiences, schools and young people, community organisations, cultural and heritage sector organisations, and the general public. - Embedding racial and social justice principles in creative collections-based engagement work. - Developing and maintaining meaningful relationships and partnerships that support engagement with the Centre’s collections. - Supporting the Centre’s mission to make Global Majority history, heritage and culture more accessible through community partnerships and inclusive practice. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Key criteria include the following: Essential Criteria: - Experience of embedding anti-racism and social justice in engagement activities. - Experience of working with collections. - Ability to develop and deliver inclusive and creative engagement activities. - Experience of working with a range of audiences and stakeholders. - Commitment to the values of anti-racism, community partnership and inclusive practice. Desirable Criteria: - Experience working within a library, archive, heritage or cultural organisation. - Experience of working with schools and young people. - Experience of community engagement and partnership working. - Knowledge of Global Majority histories, migration, refugeeism and race equality issues. - Experience of working in a hybrid environment across multiple locations. Please see the attached document for the full Person Specification. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Maya Sharma, Head of the Ahmed Iqbal Ullah RACE Centre & Education Trust Email Address: maya.sharma@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support JobTrain Support Applications close at midnight on Sunday 1 November 2026. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Please note: To be shortlisted for interview you will need to demonstrate experience of embedding anti-racism and social justice in engagement activities, and experience of working with collections. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032209 Collections Engagement Officer FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45810": {
      "title": "Divisional Administrator",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Divisional Administrator Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Divisional Administrator (Redeployees Only) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032159 Salary: £29,588 - £32,080 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Pharmacy and Optometry Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 21/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 12 months School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated, organised and collaborative individual to join our Division of Pharmacy and Optometry as a Divisional Administrator. This role provides comprehensive and proactive PA, administrative, operational, people services and finance support to staff and activities across the Division. You will help ensure the smooth running of day-to-day divisional activity by coordinating meetings, events, recruitment and administrative processes, while working professionally with colleagues and stakeholders across the University. Please note that the earliest start date for this post is Monday 2nd November 2026 You will be responsible for: Providing proactive PA and administrative support, including diary coordination, meeting arrangements, agenda preparation, minute taking and following up agreed actions. Coordinating divisional meetings, committees, seminars, visitor programmes and events, ensuring logistics, materials and related arrangements are in place. Supporting recruitment and people-related administration, including selection arrangements, interview coordination, appointments, onboarding and induction activity. Supporting finance and procurement administration, including purchasing, expenses, invoice-related activity, payment forms and liaison with finance colleagues. Maintaining effective communication with academic, research, professional services and external stakeholders, signposting queries appropriately and escalating matters where required. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate the following essential criteria: Essential Criteria Experience of working in an administrative, customer-facing role and providing effective PA support, including diary management and meeting coordination. Experience of supporting meetings, committees or events, including arranging logistics, preparing documentation, taking minutes and following up actions. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to work professionally with a wide range of colleagues and stakeholders. Strong organisational skills, with the ability to prioritise competing tasks, meet deadlines, maintain accuracy and resolve routine problems effectively. Good IT skills, particularly in Microsoft 365 applications, with a proactive, flexible and collaborative approach to supporting colleagues and responding to changing priorities. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Paula Lee Email Address: paula.lee@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt Divisional Administrator - G4 - JD BMH-032159 (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45636": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Spatiotemporal Causal Inference",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Spatiotemporal Causal Inference Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Spatiotemporal Causal Inference Apply for job Job reference: BMH-031990 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Paterson Building Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Cancer Sciences Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 22/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed-term for up to 34 months, ending no later than 30th September 2029 School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description This is an outstanding opportunity for a Research Associate, with a background in statistics, biostatistics, data science, computer science, applied mathematics, or a related quantitative discipline, to work within the reirradiation research team under the supervision of Dr Vasquez Osorio. The post holder will be part of the Radiotherapy Related Research (RRR) group, which is based at the Paterson Building in Withington. The candidate will support the aims of Dr Vasquez Osorio’s UKRI Future Leaders Fellowship on Advancing Reirradiation and Treatment Outcome Understanding with Robust Dose Mapping and Causal Inference. This innovative project will study how advanced spatiotemporal causal inference methods can be developed and applied to complex radiotherapy datasets to understand the relationship between radiation dose and treatment outcomes, enabling improved evidence-based decision-making for patients undergoing reirradiation. This position offers an ideal opportunity for an enthusiastic, well-motivated individual to work as part of a sociable multidisciplinary team consisting of physicists, computer scientists, clinicians, radiographers, and imaging experts. The ideal candidate will have excellent interpersonal, communication, programming, and organisational skills, and will be able to work with minimal supervision. Experience of advanced statistical modelling, causal inference methods, and programming for the analysis of complex datasets is essential for the position. You will be responsible for: developing research objectives and proposals for own or joint research, with the assistance of a mentor if required. conducting individual and collaborative research projects. writing up research work for publication. communicating complex information, orally, in writing and electronically. using creativity to analyse and interpret research data and draw conclusions on the outcomes. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria having, or being about to obtain, a relevant PhD (or equivalent). experience in advanced statistical modelling and/or causal inference methods. ability to develop or adapt novel computational or statistical methods (beyond application of existing techniques). strong programming skills (e.g. Python and/or R). knowledge of radiotherapy, cancer research, medical imaging, or clinical data analysis. Desirable Criteria experience analysing complex biomedical, healthcare, or spatiotemporal datasets. experience with spatiotemporal methods. track record of presenting at national and international conferences. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr Eliana Vasquez Osorio / Dr Ruth Forman Email Address: Eliana.vasquezosorio@manchester.ac.uk / ruth.forman@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45430": {
      "title": "Postgraduate Research Administrator - Professional Doctorate in Clinical Science",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Postgraduate Research Administrator - Professional Doctorate in Clinical Science Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Postgraduate Research Administrator - Professional Doctorate in Clinical Science Apply for job Job reference: BMH-031791 Salary: £29,588 - £32,080 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FBMH Research and Business Engagement Support Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 14/10/2026 Contract Duration: 3 years School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Postgraduate Research Administrator (Professional Doctorate in Clinical Science). This role offers supports the delivery of the Postgraduate Research (PGR) Professional Doctorate in Clinical Science programme within the Doctoral Academy, ensuring the efficient, consistent and compliant administration of student processes. The programme forms the academic component of the Higher Specialist Scientist Training (HSST) programme, delivered in partnership with the National School for Healthcare Science and commissioned by the National Health Service (NHS). Trainees undertake the programme on a 5-year part-time basis alongside their NHS roles. You will be responsible for: Administering programme and activity support for the faculty’s professional doctorate in Clinical Sciences programme. Providing administrative support to students undertaking taught units as part of their programme, monitoring student engagement where required and escalating issues where appropriate. Maintaining accurate records and ensuring data quality and data protection across systems. Maintaining compliance with university governance arrangements, policies, and the regulatory obligations imposed by relevant external bodies. Coordinating research project activity for PGRs, liaising with Programme Directors and supervisory teams (internal and external), monitoring progress, organising oral assessments and working with the Exams Team as required. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Experience of working in an administrative role, ideally within higher or further education or a comparable environment supporting students and programme-based activity. An understanding of student or programme administration within a university or similarly complex organisation (e.g. NHS) would be advantageous, though not essential. Strong written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to provide advice and guidance to a range of stakeholders. Excellent organisational skills, with the ability to prioritise a varied workload and meet deadlines. Ability to work collaboratively as part of a team and respond flexibly to changing priorities. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . Please note, this role has a requirement to work on campus 3 days per week, as it is part of a student facing team. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Alison Cox Email Address: alison.cox@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-031791 Research Administrator - Professional Doctorate in Clinical Science FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45470": {
      "title": "Research Associate in vivo imaging",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in vivo imaging Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in vivo imaging Apply for job Job reference: BMH-031830 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Cardiovascular Sciences Hours Per Week: Full Time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 31/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed Term for 36 months School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description This is an outstanding opportunity for a Research Associate with expertise in pre-clinical in-vivo cerebrovascular imaging, vascular physiology or neurovascular coupling to join the Microvascular@Manchester group under the supervision of Adam Greenstein, Ingo Schiessl, Kira Shaw and Harry Pritchard. The post holder will be based within the Division of Cardiovascular Sciences and the Geoffrey Jefferson Brain Research Centre at the University of Manchester. The position is funded through the Manchester NaVigate Partnership, a major collaborative programme investigating how impaired cerebral blood flow contributes to cognitive decline and dementia. The project will determine how vascular amyloid-β(1-40) alters vascular smooth muscle calcium signaling, arterial constriction and cerebral blood flow, and whether these mechanisms can be targeted therapeutically. The successful candidate will lead studies using state-of-the-art pre- clinical in vivo imaging approaches, including multiphoton microscopy and optical imaging methods, to assess pial artery function, vascular smooth muscle calcium signaling and cerebral blood flow in mouse models relevant to Alzheimer’s disease. Complementary approaches will include behavioural assessment, histology, immunohistochemistry and physiological analysis. This position offers an excellent opportunity for an ambitious and highly motivated researcher to lead an exciting translational project within a multidisciplinary academic-industry collaboration. The ideal candidate will have strong experimental and analytical skills, experience in preclinical vascular imaging, and a passion for understanding mechanisms underlying neurovascular dysfunction in dementia. The successful applicant will have opportunities to publish, present internationally, develop collaborations and contribute to future grant applications within The University of Manchester. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Successful candidates may be subject to pre-employment screening carried out on our behalf by a third party. The offer of employment will be dependent on the successful candidate passing that screening. Whilst you will be required to provide express consent at a later stage, by continuing with your application now you acknowledge that you are aware that such screening will take place, and agree to take part in the process. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr. Ingo Schiessl Email: i.schiessl@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Please see Further Particulars and Person Specification below Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=34459": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Digital Engineering for Fusion Energy",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Lecturer in Digital Engineering for Fusion Energy – (Teaching and Research) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Lecturer in Digital Engineering for Fusion Energy – (Teaching and Research) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-030868 Salary: £47,389 - £58,225 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 31/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking an exceptional candidate to join the Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering as a Lecturer in Digital Engineering for Fusion Energy. The role-holder will drive forward research and teaching in digital engineering for fusion energy, developing and delivering the computational tools needed to meet the formidable engineering challenges this exciting field will face over the coming decades. You will be responsible for: Development and delivery of internationally excellent research in one or more of the following topics, from an engineering perspective: multiphysics modelling and simulation of fusion in-vessel components (breeder blanket, first wall and divertor); development of digital twins, high-performance computing and AI/machine learning for fusion design and operation; verification, validation and uncertainty quantification for fusion safety and licensing; development of computational engineering tools for fusion materials modelling and structural integrity assessment Undertaking teaching duties in areas related to modelling & simulation, mechanics, structures & engineering design, (including but not limited to assessment, academic advising and project supervision) within the University on undergraduate and postgraduate courses. Embedding social responsibility and environmental sustainability within teaching and research practice. About You Essential Criteria Educated to PhD level or equivalent in a topic closely linked to digital engineering for fusion energy, most likely via undergraduate degree in engineering, maths or physics. Proven track record in one of the areas of digital engineering for fusion energy described in the above section. Excellent communication skills and evidence of developing/delivering successful collaboration with other academic researchers. Commitment to contributing improvements to advance our inclusive, equal and fair working environment. Desirable Criteria Experience with or capability for establishing meaningful links with industrial partners which are directly or indirectly relevant to the fusion or nuclear energy sector. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Lee Margetts Email Address: lee.margetts@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    }
